./paper/PAPER_IMPROVEMENT_LOG.md:9:## Review Findings
./paper/PAPER_IMPROVEMENT_LOG.md:13:- `MAJOR` The experiments section described the calibration split as being used for "threshold tuning," which is conceptually wrong for an AUROC-centric paper and also conflicted with the method section, which uses calibration only for family-adaptive fusion weights. This was corrected at `sections/4_experiments.tex:14` and aligned with `sections/3_method.tex:157-160`.
./paper/PAPER_IMPROVEMENT_LOG.md:25:- `MAJOR` AUROC deltas were expressed as percentages in places where the manuscript is actually reporting absolute AUROC-point improvements. This is imprecise and can be read as a relative improvement claim. I converted these to AUROC points at `sections/1_introduction.tex:20,63`, `sections/4_experiments.tex:119,121,155-156,165,185`, and kept percentage notation only for actual call-rate or cost changes.
./paper/PAPER_IMPROVEMENT_LOG.md:45:  Reframed prior-work citations, softened overclaims, converted AUROC deltas to points, inserted a visible architecture placeholder, and aligned benchmark/calibration counts.
./paper/PAPER_IMPROVEMENT_LOG.md:54:  Harmonized the benchmark description, corrected the domain-win count, corrected the D9 family-analysis number, softened causal language, converted AUROC deltas to points, added a visible Pareto placeholder, and replaced impossible failure-analysis counts with a verifiable summary.
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:14:\paragraph{Framework vs.\ headline recipe.}
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:18:a concrete instance of this framework, the v6 ReAct agent ensembled
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:20:adds the v8 refutation variant as an interpretable alternative with a
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:40:    \item[\textbf{AnomalyVFM}~\citep{anomalyvfm2025}.] Vision foundation model adapted via LoRA for zero-shot anomaly segmentation, with a convolutional decoder. We use the DINOv2 backbone variant; provides image-level score plus pixel mask. Complementary to SubspaceAD on infrastructure (calibration AUROC $0.72$ on D4 vs SubspaceAD's $0.50$) and on MVTec-LOCO logical anomalies where its synthetic-anomaly training data covers compositional defects.
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:49:    \item[\textbf{expert-only}.] No VLM call; the expert score is sigmoid-rescaled around the calibration median and used directly. Selected for domains where the calibration AUROC of the expert exceeds the best VLM-based strategy by $\ge 3$ pp (e.g.\ D7 GI endoscopy with SubspaceAD).
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:56:For each domain $d$, the agent selects a (\emph{strategy}, \emph{expert}) pair $\pi(d)$ from the joint catalogue. We use a \textbf{calibration argmax with a fusion-default}: the default plan is \emph{fusion(SubspaceAD)}, and we override only when the calibration AUROC of an alternative exceeds the default by at least $3$ pp (the ``override margin''). The 3-pp margin is itself frozen from a one-time sweep on a held-out backbone (GPT-5.4 calibration); we do \emph{not} retune it per backbone. Rationale: $20$ calibration items have a per-domain bootstrap noise floor of $\sim 2$ pp, so a margin slightly above the noise floor avoids over-fitting to small-sample noise.
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:60:\paragraph{Why this works.} Three single-strategy regimes map to different per-domain optima: \emph{fusion(SubspaceAD)} dominates texture / few-shot industrial, retail, brain MRI, liver CT, dermoscopy; \emph{direct} wins on semantic domains where the expert distorts the score (LEVIR change, SDNET infrastructure, road obstacle); \emph{expert-only} wins on domains where the expert is near-perfect (GI endoscopy with SubspaceAD AUROC $0.97$). A single fixed strategy cannot capture all three regimes.
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:89:\subsection{The v6 ReAct agent (headline system)}
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:94:different concrete instance, the \textbf{v6 ReAct agent} ensembled with
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:112:($+0.3$ pp) but falls short on SeedVL ($-1.7$ pp). Our headline recipe
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:120:\subsection{Refutation agent (v8)}
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:123:Beyond the calibration-argmax planner, we introduce a deliberation protocol that directly targets two failure modes we observe in v6.5: (i) \emph{coarse rank grid}---the VLM emits scores drawn from a small set of unique values (11 distinct values on the Qwen3.5-VL test set), which limits ranking resolution and caps AUROC gains; (ii) \emph{confirmation bias}---tool calls tend to support the VLM's working hypothesis rather than test it.
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:126:The refutation agent runs on top of the v6 ReAct loop but replaces the free-form ``call any tool, update score'' pattern with a structured three-phase schema:
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:134:In v6.5, a tool was invoked to \emph{support} the VLM's working hypothesis and the system accumulated supporting observations. In v8, every tool output has exactly one legitimate use---retiring a candidate feature---so the protocol is naturally biased toward ruling anomalies \emph{out}. The structured output (\texttt{candidate\_features}, per-turn \texttt{refutation\_verdict}, \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features}) gives a reviewer an auditable trace.
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:137:Both v6.5 and v8 standalone sit below Direct on dev (dev macros $0.6942$ and $0.6865$ vs Direct $0.7599$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B). We therefore report the agents after averaging with the Direct VLM score at a single frozen weight selected on dev:
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:141:with $w{=}0.5$ for the v6.5 ensemble and $w{=}0.6$ for the v8 ensemble. Section~\ref{sec:experiments} contains the ablation over $w$. A triple blend $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.1\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5} + 0.4\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ is also reported; all weights are frozen on dev before a single test query is seen.
./paper/sections/3_method.tex:144:Section~\ref{sec:experiments} isolates the causal driver of the ensemble gain via rank-preserving and rank-collapsing transformations. Direct emits only $11$ unique scores on our test set, v6.5's free-form JSON protocol emits $49$, and averaging the two fills in the rank ties inside Direct's coarse bimodal clusters with real signal. Transformations that preserve the agent's $49$ unique values---whether keeping the middle-zone mass at $24$\% (the original), inflating it to $100$\% (affine compression), or collapsing it to $0$\% (rank-preserving extreme remap)---all reach the same $+4.7$~pp ensemble gain. A median-split binarisation that collapses the $49$ values to $2$ cuts the gain in half. v8's explicit refutation schema, which assigns scores from a small vocabulary tied to the refutation-verdict decision, retains fewer unique values and therefore captures a smaller share of the ensemble gain. Designing an agent whose deliberation mechanism \emph{also} produces a fine rank grid is the main open direction left by this work.
