./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1319:/bin/bash -lc "rg -n \"Direct|AnomalyClaw|AUROC|rank|granularity|MMAD|MCQ|refutation|v8|SeedVL|Qwen|GPT|0\\.8|0\\.813|0\\.864|0\\.801|0\\.814|1418|989|0\\.731|0\\.708\" paper/sections" in /hdd1/jiangxi/AD-Agent
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1321:paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:4:We introduced \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free visual anomaly detection framework whose headline method is score-diverse VLM-agent ensembling: a simple arithmetic average of a Direct VLM score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score. On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} ($1{,}418$ test items, 12 domains) the ensemble reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating descriptor-only Direct by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). On GPT-5.4 it reaches $0.864$, edging the SubspaceAD-fusion baseline by $+0.9$ pp while using no external expert model. We localise the mechanism to \emph{score-rank granularity}: Direct emits $11$ unique scores on the test set, the agent emits $49$; averaging breaks ties inside Direct's coarse bimodal clusters with the agent's finer rank information. Controlled rank-preserving transformations confirm this: $0\%$ middle-mass with $49$ unique values still delivers $+4.68$ pp, whereas $0\%$ middle-mass with only $2$ unique values delivers $+2.31$ pp.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1322:paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:6:We also introduced a \emph{refutation agent} (v8) that instantiates a ``normal until survived refutation'' protocol: the VLM lists candidate features, a refutation tool checks each against the reference pool, surviving features count as anomaly evidence. v8 ensembled with Direct gains $+1.28$ pp on Qwen3.5 test (positive one-sided bootstrap probability $0.962$; 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$ touches zero). v8 is positioned as an interpretable variant, not the main method: it produces per-item traces (candidate list, refutation verdict per tool call, remaining candidates) that a reviewer can audit, at the cost of $3.3$ pp relative to the plain $0.5$-average v6 ensemble. The smaller v8 gain is consistent with its coarser rank grid, closing the loop between deliberation-protocol design and the rank-granularity mechanism.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1323:paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:11:The v6 ensemble's macro-AUROC gain over SubspaceAD fusion is positive only on GPT-5.4 ($+0.9$ pp) and is tied on Qwen3.5 ($\pm 0$ pp). The refutation agent's Qwen3.5 test gain is positive-bootstrap but its CI touches zero; its stored test JSON was produced before a minor schema fix (corrected code is released; re-run not executed). GPT-5.4 v8 test at scale repeatedly rate-limits our proprietary API gateway, so GPT-5.4 numbers for v8 are dev-only ($n{=}480$). The v8 ensemble claim therefore generalises across backbones only at dev-split resolution, not test. The agent+Direct recipe matches the SubspaceAD-fusion baseline on Qwen3.5 rather than strictly outperforming it; we report it as a VLM-only alternative that avoids a domain-specialised expert.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1324:paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:14:Three directions follow from the rank-granularity mechanism: (i) deliberation protocols designed for a fine rank grid (e.g.\ requiring the VLM to output continuous refutation-strength scores rather than verdict categories) should improve v8's ensemble gain; (ii) a principled calibration-split ensemble weight selector per backbone, rather than our dev-frozen $w$; (iii) extension of the refutation protocol to operate on multiple candidate-feature lists produced from different prompt seeds, combining internal hypothesis diversity with our cross-system ensemble.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1325:paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:3:\textbf{Main finding.} On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items), a simple arithmetic ensemble of the VLM's Direct score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline $0.7684$ by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The ensemble recipe is $s_{\mathrm{final}} = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, \emph{frozen} on dev before any test query is seen. The recipe beats the stronger SubspaceAD \emph{fusion} baseline by $+0.9$ pp on GPT-5.4 and matches it on Qwen3.5, using only VLM outputs, no additional expert model.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1326:paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:5:\textbf{Mechanism.} Controlled rank-preserving ablations localise the gain to \emph{score-rank granularity}, not to middle-zone mass. Direct emits only 11 unique score values on our test set whereas the v6 ReAct agent emits 49; the agent's finer rank grid breaks ties inside Direct's bimodal clusters when the two scores are averaged. A rank-preserving transformation that keeps the agent's 49 unique scores but pushes them all outside $[0.2, 0.8]$ preserves the ensemble gain ($+4.68$ pp) despite $0\%$ middle-zone mass; a rank-collapsing binarisation (two unique values) drops the gain to $+2.31$ pp; a random middle-mass noise partner (uniform in $[0.2, 0.8]$) gives $-3.4$ pp. Middle-zone mass is therefore correlated with ensemble gain only because it correlates with rank granularity; when we decouple them, granularity is what matters. The deeper ingredients are: (i) the agent's score has non-trivial standalone signal (random score with no signal hurts the ensemble); (ii) the agent has a distinct rank grid with many unique values; (iii) the agent's errors are complementary to the VLM's.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1327:paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:7:\textbf{Secondary contribution.} We introduce a \emph{refutation agent} (v8) that implements an explicit ``normal until survived refutation'' protocol: the VLM lists candidate features, each is targeted by a refutation tool, surviving candidates yield anomaly evidence. v8 is interpretable---a reviewer can audit exactly which features survived and why---and gains $+1.28$ pp on Qwen3.5 test (CI $[-0.001,+0.027]$) when ensembled with Direct. Its smaller gain relative to v6 is explained by the same rank-granularity mechanism: v8's explicit refutation protocol collapses scores to a small number of unique values ($0.05, 0.85$) which preserves correct rank on refuted vs surviving features but destroys rank granularity within each group.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1328:paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:9:\textbf{Takeaway.} Training-free VAD benefits from \emph{score-diverse} VLM-agent ensembling; the gain is explained by complementary rank structure, not by stronger standalone agents. We release the v6/v8 agent implementations, per-tool single-tool audit framework (13 tools, dev n=480), and full paired-bootstrap analysis artifacts.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1329:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:8:We evaluate on \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11}, spanning twelve domain codes grouped into 11 reported families: D1 MVTec-AD industrial, D2 GoodsAD retail, D3 SDNET2018 concrete infrastructure (dev only), D4 DermaMNIST dermoscopy, D5 BraTS2021 brain MRI, D5b BMAD brain MRI, D5c BMAD liver CT, D5d HyperKvasir GI endoscopy variant, D6 LEVIR-CD+ remote-sensing change, D7 HyperKvasir GI endoscopy, D8 RA21+BDD road, D9 MVTec-LOCO logical, D10 VisA industrial. Each domain contributes 20 calibration / 40 dev / 120 test items (D8: 98 test). All items are reference-based, paired with 1--10 normal references. Total test items $n{=}1418$ (fusion strategies drop D8 items lacking SubspaceAD support to $n{=}1298$ and fall back to direct on the rest). The full per-domain specification is in Appendix~\ref{app:benchmark}.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1330:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:11:Three VLMs spanning proprietary--open: \textbf{GPT-5.4} (proprietary frontier), \textbf{SeedVL} (\texttt{doubao-seed-2-0-lite}, proprietary non-frontier), and \textbf{Qwen3.5-VL-27B} (open-weight, served locally via vLLM, thinking disabled). Temperature $0$, identical prompts.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1332:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:17:Per-domain AUROC; macro-averaged across the 12 domain codes present in test. All thresholds and router assignments frozen from calibration. VLM anomaly scores extracted via structured JSON output (schema in Appendix~\ref{app:scoring}); $s = c$ if label is ``anomalous'' else $1 - c$. Bootstrap 95\% CIs via stratified paired bootstrap (per-domain stratification, $1{,}000$ resamples).
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1333:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:24:\caption{\textbf{Main results: per-domain AUROC across three VLM backbones} (test split, $n{=}1{,}418$). For each backbone we report the descriptor-only Direct VLM and our \emph{agent+Direct ensemble} ($s = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, weights frozen on dev). Agent choice per backbone: v6.5 on Qwen3.5/SeedVL, v6.6 self-ensemble on GPT-5.4 (selected on dev). \textbf{Macro} row at the bottom; $\Delta$ is ensemble macro minus Direct macro. 95\% paired bootstrap CIs on macro $\Delta$ are reported under the table; all three backbones show $P(\Delta\!>\!0)\!\geq\!0.999$.}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1334:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:30: & \multicolumn{3}{c}{GPT-5.4} & & \multicolumn{3}{c}{SeedVL} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Qwen3.5-VL-27B} \\
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1346:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:53:SeedVL $[+0.8, +3.5]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0)\!=\!0.999$;\;
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1348:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:55:For reference, the descriptor-only Direct VLM and the SubspaceAD-fusion baseline (single call; $s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.2\,\sigma(s_\mathrm{SubspaceAD})$) have macro AUROC $0.855/0.808/0.814$ on GPT-5.4/SeedVL/Qwen3.5; the agent+Direct ensemble reaches $0.864/0.801/0.814$: $+0.87/{-}0.6/\pm 0$\,pp vs the expert-assisted fusion baseline, using \emph{no} external expert model. Per-backbone single-system agent results (v6.5 or v6.6 alone, no ensemble), v8 refutation ensemble, and the triple blend are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}.}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1350:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:68:Replacing a generic domain descriptor with a task-anchored one significantly improves all three backbones at zero inference cost (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a): macro AUROC moves from 0.761 to 0.825 on GPT-5.4 ($+6.4$ pp, 95\% CI $[+4.4,+8.4]$), 0.748 to 0.789 on SeedVL ($+4.1$ pp, $[+1.6,+6.4]$), and 0.760 to 0.792 on Qwen3.5 ($+3.2$ pp, $[+1.0,+5.5]$). The gain concentrates in domains where VLM world-knowledge priors conflict with the task-specific anomaly definition (D08 LEVIR change, D06 BMAD liver CT); full per-backbone per-domain evidence is in Appendix~\ref{app:descriptor}. We recommend this as baseline practice for any training-free VAD system.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1351:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:71:Across 12 test domain codes, the argmax strategy differs by domain: fusion is preferred on 7--8 domains (industrial, retail, logical, liver CT, brain MRI), direct wins on 2--3 (brain MRI on SeedVL, LEVIR change, road), debate wins on 1--2 (road on GPT-5.4), and expert-only wins on 1 (dermoscopy via patch-$k$-NN). Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}(a) visualises this on SeedVL; the per-backbone tables are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. This strategy variance is the motivation for an agent rather than a single fixed recipe.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1352:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:74:The calibration router (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}b) matches or beats every single-strategy baseline on every backbone. On SeedVL it beats fusion by \textbf{$+1.64$ pp} (95\% CI $[+0.02, +3.18]$, significant at $p{<}0.05$ by stratified paired bootstrap); it beats direct by $+3.27$ pp on SeedVL, $+0.97$ pp on GPT-5.4, and $+5.63$ pp on Qwen3.5 (all significant except GPT-5.4 $\to$ direct). The oracle upper bound leaves $+1.3$, $+1.3$, $+1.4$ pp of further headroom on the three backbones respectively. A zero-data \emph{descriptor} router (rules from the benchmark family taxonomy, no metrics consulted) is surprisingly competitive: $+1.7$ / $+2.2$ / $+4.9$ pp over direct on GPT-5.4 / SeedVL / Qwen3.5, all significant.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1353:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:79:\paragraph{Finding 5: The real autonomous agent + Direct VLM ensemble is the winning recipe.}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1354:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:80:Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs the stronger SubspaceAD-fusion baseline), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation; $\pm 0$ vs fusion, i.e.\ matches fusion with no expert model required), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp over Direct, $+0.14$ vs fusion, not significant). The scientific content of the comparison is ``VLM-only ensemble edges fusion on GPT-5.4 and matches fusion on Qwen3.5, without the SubspaceAD expert''---the right target to foreground because SubspaceAD is already a strong training-free baseline (\S\ref{ssec:setup}). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by $+3$--$6$ pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1355:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:82:\paragraph{Finding 6: The refutation agent (v8) is an interpretable alternative with a smaller but significant ensemble gain.}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1356:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:83:\label{ssec:v8_finding}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1357:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:84:Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$; one-sided bootstrap probability $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). Because the CI touches zero, we characterise this gain as positive-bootstrap rather than strictly significant. On GPT-5.4 dev (no test due to proprietary API rate-limit issues for concurrent v8 runs), the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). A minor schema fix was applied to the v8 runner after this test-set inference was completed; the reported v8 AUROC therefore reflects the pre-fix run. The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1361:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:93:    \item \textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}: monotone-remap v6 scores to linearly spaced points in $[0.05, 0.19]\cup[0.81, 0.95]$, preserving every unique rank but moving all 49 values outside $[0.2, 0.8]$. Preserves rank granularity AND standalone AUROC; destroys middle-zone mass.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1363:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:95:    \item \textbf{v8-SOFT}: monotone compression of v8 scores into $[0.25, 0.75]$. Adds middle-zone mass while preserving rank.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1365:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:101:\caption{Controlled ablation on Qwen3.5-VL-27B test ($n{=}1418$, macro AUROC over 12 domains). Columns: the agent variant, its standalone AUROC, the $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}+0.5\!\cdot\!X$ ensemble AUROC, the \emph{number of unique score values}, the middle-zone mass, and the Spearman correlation with Direct. The key row is \textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}: middle-mass drops to $0\%$ but rank granularity is preserved, and the ensemble gain actually \emph{increases} to $+4.68$ pp. Conversely \textbf{v6-BIN} keeps sign-level direction but collapses ranks from $49$ to $2$, and the ensemble drops to $+2.31$ pp. Middle-mass is correlational with rank granularity on unprocessed outputs; rank granularity is what causes ensemble gain.}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1366:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:114:v8 original           & $0.6710$ & $+1.22$ & $\sim\!15$ & $0.4\%$ & $0.477$ & $0.594$ \\
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1367:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:115:v8-SOFT               & $0.6710$ & $+1.35$ & $\sim\!15$ & $100\%$ & $0.477$ & $0.634$ \\
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1371:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:127:The calibration router's choice coincides with the oracle on 9/12 domains for GPT-5.4 and 10/12 for both SeedVL and Qwen3.5. Mismatches concentrate where two strategies are within 1 pp on calibration (a bootstrap-level tie that the router cannot reliably resolve with 20 items), and the realised AUROC gap on test is small ($\le 0.06$). The per-backbone per-strategy per-domain table and the per-domain router-choice overlay are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain} (Figure~\ref{fig:per_domain_appendix}).
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1372:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:134:\caption{Stratified paired bootstrap summary (test split, $1{,}000$ resamples, per-domain stratification). $\Delta$ is the pairwise macro-AUROC gap; CI is the 95\% percentile interval; \checkmark\ means the CI excludes zero.}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1377:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:146:SeedVL & descriptor-router vs direct & $+0.022$ & $[+0.014, +0.031]$ & \checkmark \\
