./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1379:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:148:SeedVL & fusion vs direct & $+0.016$ & $[+0.006, +0.027]$ & \checkmark \\
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1380:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:149:SeedVL & oracle vs calibration-router & $+0.013$ & $[+0.001, +0.025]$ & \checkmark \\
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1385:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:159:Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap} summarises the stratified paired bootstrap for the headline comparisons. The calibration router strictly beats every single-strategy baseline on SeedVL (including fusion, significant); it ties fusion on GPT-5.4 and Qwen3.5 (fusion is the dominant single strategy on those backbones). Against the weaker \emph{direct} baseline that prior agentic-VAD work usually compares to, all three routers---descriptor, calibration, and oracle---show significant gains.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1386:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:161:\subsection{Cross-benchmark generalisation: MMAD}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1387:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:164:We re-evaluated our Qwen3.5-VL-27B agent+Direct ensemble on \textbf{MMAD}~\citep{jiang2024mmad}, a $39{,}670$-question industrial-inspection MCQ benchmark whose Anomaly-Detection subset contains $8{,}297$ Yes/No binary-judgment questions over MVTec-AD, MVTec-LOCO, VisA, and GoodsAD. We report on a stratified $989$-item sample balanced across all $38$ product classes (5--$\sim$50 items per class, seed$=42$). Each query uses the first four \texttt{random\_templates} from \texttt{mmad.json} as normal references (matching MMAD's few-shot$=4$ setup); the Qwen3.5-VL-27B agent is the same unmodified \texttt{agent\_v6.py} used in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, and the ensemble is the same $0.5$-average recipe with no re-tuning.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1388:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:168:\caption{\textbf{MMAD cross-benchmark transfer} (Qwen3.5-VL-27B, stratified $n{=}989$ across $38$ classes, Anomaly-Detection subset). We report both the MMAD MCQ accuracy (A=Yes/B=No answer from score${>}0.5$) and the AUROC that is natively comparable to Table~\ref{tab:main_results}. AUROC gain transfers ($+2.31$ pp pooled, $95\%$ CI $[+0.8,+3.9]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}0.996$); MCQ accuracy does \emph{not} gain, consistent with the rank-granularity mechanism of \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity}: averaging two scores changes rank ordering but rarely flips a score's sign relative to $0.5$.}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1389:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:173: & \multicolumn{3}{c}{MCQ Accuracy (\%)} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Score AUROC} \\
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1396:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:188:{\footnotesize GoodsAD and MVTec-AD do not report AUROC because the stratified $989$-item sample has only one label class present for these subsets (GoodsAD: all 347 items label$=$1 because the subset has no dedicated ``good'' folder; MVTec-AD: $n{=}5$ sample is too small). The subset-level accuracy is reported without AUROC. MCQ Accuracy uses the mapping score${>}0.5 \rightarrow$A, else B.}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1397:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:191:Two findings follow. First, the AUROC gain transfers: pooled AUROC moves from $0.708$ (Direct) to $0.731$ (agent$+$Direct ensemble), $+2.31$ pp with $95\%$ CI $[+0.8, +3.9]$ on a paired bootstrap---the same direction and comparable magnitude as Table~\ref{tab:main_results}'s Qwen3.5 test gain. Second, MCQ accuracy does \emph{not} gain ($-0.8$ pp vs Direct). This is consistent with \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity}: averaging scores changes the \emph{rank order} between positive and negative items (helps AUROC) but rarely crosses the decision threshold at $0.5$ (does not help accuracy). A method that needs to improve threshold-based accuracy would need, in addition, a calibrated threshold or a decision rule that exploits the agent's disagreement with Direct near $0.5$; we leave that to future work. The important conclusion is that the ensemble mechanism is benchmark-agnostic at the \emph{ranking} level: on a completely independent benchmark, with different reference-template construction and class taxonomy, it delivers the same AUROC improvement at no inference-time re-tuning.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1398:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:194:All hyperparameters are frozen from the 20-item-per-domain calibration split and never re-tuned on dev/test: fusion weight $w{=}0.2$ (selected on GPT-5.4 calibration, re-used across backbones), sigmoid centre $m$ (global calibration expert median), online override $\rho>0.8$, descriptor-router rules (no metrics consulted), and calibration-router argmax. Leave-one-domain-out: the calibration-router--fusion gap is positive on 10/12 splits for SeedVL ($-0.8/+2.5/0.79$ pp min/max/std) and 9/12 for Qwen3.5; full table Appendix~\ref{app:loo}.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1400:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:203:The agent's maximum possible gain is bounded by how often different strategies disagree with ground truth in opposite directions. On the SeedVL test split, D5c liver CT and D4 dermoscopy have the largest expert-rescue rows (26\% / 21\% expert-only-correct over direct), while D5d GI and D6 LEVIR have large direct-only-correct rows (46\% / 38\%), matching the router's assignments. Average VLM budget for the calibration router is $\approx 1.0$ calls/image; the online $\rho$-override escalates $\sim 12\%$ of items, accounting for the whole gap to the 1.3-call bound. Full correctness matrix and routing distribution: Appendix~\ref{app:complementarity}, \ref{app:routing}.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1401:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:207:The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1402:paper/sections/4x_mmad_fulltype.tex:2:% Will be spliced into 4_experiments.tex after \subsection{Cross-benchmark generalisation: MMAD}.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1403:paper/sections/4x_mmad_fulltype.tex:4:\subsection{A unified task-aware agent for MMAD}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1404:paper/sections/4x_mmad_fulltype.tex:7:The cross-benchmark MMAD evaluation in \S\ref{ssec:mmad} used only the
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1405:paper/sections/4x_mmad_fulltype.tex:8:Anomaly-Detection subset (Yes/No). MMAD additionally contains eight
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1407:paper/sections/4x_mmad_fulltype.tex:20:CrossDomainVAD also answers MCQ items on MMAD.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1411:paper/sections/4x_mmad_fulltype.tex:35:\caption{\textbf{MMAD full-type MCQ accuracy} (Qwen3.5-VL-27B, test
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1414:paper/sections/4x_mmad_fulltype.tex:44:Question type ($n$)            & Direct VLM & Unified Agent v9 & Ensemble & Ens.~(per-class $\tau$) \\
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1416:paper/sections/4x_mmad_fulltype.tex:67:%    retaining the AUROC gain of \S\ref{ssec:mmad}.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1421:paper/sections/2_related_work.tex:8:Recent work uses large VLMs directly for anomaly decisions. AnomalyGPT~\citep{gu2024anomalygpt} fine-tunes a VLM on industrial defect data with simulated anomalies; MMAD~\citep{jiang2024mmad} evaluates several frontier VLMs on a multi-task anomaly benchmark. The dominant deployment pattern, however, remains a \emph{single forward pass} with a short prompt asking whether the image is abnormal, optionally with one or two reference images. As we show in \S\ref{sec:experiments}, this pattern is substantially under-calibrated when paired with generic domain descriptions: a one-sentence task-anchored descriptor alone moves a frontier VLM from $0.761$ to $0.825$ macro AUROC. AnomalyClaw stays on top of such a VLM without any fine-tuning and treats the descriptor-enhanced single-pass call as its first phase.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1424:paper/sections/2_related_work.tex:23:Our contribution is (i) a decoupled Tool-$\times$-Expert-$\times$-Strategy framework for cross-domain VAD, (ii) a calibration-only router that outperforms every single-strategy baseline on all three backbones (significantly on SeedVL), (iii) an empirical study of what moves training-free VAD performance on top of modern VLMs, and (iv) a benchmark and a documented protocol to make such claims auditable.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1426:paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:14:Two observations drive this work. First, a cross-domain VAD deployment must handle textural industrial defects, semantic road obstacles, brain-MRI lesions, and infrastructure cracks with the same pipeline. The same fixed recipe---no matter how clever---cannot be optimal on all of them simultaneously: we find that on our 12-domain test split, the argmax strategy varies by domain family and gains of up to $+3$ pp macro AUROC are available simply by switching strategy per domain (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a). Second, recent ``agentic VAD'' systems~\citep{zhang2025agentiad,chen2026eagle,wei2025autoiad} bundle three decisions---what the VLM attends to, which expert provides evidence, and how to aggregate---into a single monolithic pipeline that is hard to ablate and hard to compare against simpler baselines.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1428:paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:28:    \item \textbf{Score-diverse VLM-agent ensembling is the headline method.} A simple arithmetic average $s_{\mathrm{final}}{=}0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}}+0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$ reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B test ($n{=}1{,}418$, 12 domains), beating descriptor-only Direct by $+4.53$~pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The recipe matches SubspaceAD fusion on Qwen3.5 and edges it by $+0.9$~pp on GPT-5.4, using only VLM outputs.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1430:paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:30:    \item \textbf{Refutation agent (v8) as interpretable variant.} A ``normal until survived refutation'' protocol produces auditable per-item traces and gains $+1.28$~pp on Qwen3.5 test when ensembled with Direct. Its smaller gain is consistent with its coarser rank grid, connecting deliberation-protocol design to the rank-granularity mechanism.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1431:paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:31:    \item \textbf{Task-anchored domain descriptors.} Descriptors significantly improve all three backbones at zero inference cost ($+6.4/+4.1/+3.2$ pp on GPT-5.4, SeedVL, Qwen3.5-VL-27B; paired bootstrap, $n{=}1{,}418$, all 95\% CIs exclude zero).
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1432:paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:32:    \item \textbf{Benchmark and framework release.} We release \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domain codes, $1{,}418$ test items, calibration/dev/test splits), the Tool/Expert/Strategy registry, the calibration-argmax router, the v8 refutation implementation with full trace logging, a 13-tool single-tool audit on Qwen3.5 dev, six failed-variant ablations, and stratified paired bootstrap confidence intervals for every headline comparison.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1436:paper/sections/3_method.tex:30:    \item[\textbf{AnomalyVFM}~\citep{anomalyvfm2025}.] Vision foundation model adapted via LoRA for zero-shot anomaly segmentation, with a convolutional decoder. We use the DINOv2 backbone variant; provides image-level score plus pixel mask. Complementary to SubspaceAD on infrastructure (calibration AUROC $0.72$ on D4 vs SubspaceAD's $0.50$) and on MVTec-LOCO logical anomalies where its synthetic-anomaly training data covers compositional defects.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1437:paper/sections/3_method.tex:39:    \item[\textbf{expert-only}.] No VLM call; the expert score is sigmoid-rescaled around the calibration median and used directly. Selected for domains where the calibration AUROC of the expert exceeds the best VLM-based strategy by $\ge 3$ pp (e.g.\ D7 GI endoscopy with SubspaceAD).
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1438:paper/sections/3_method.tex:46:For each domain $d$, the agent selects a (\emph{strategy}, \emph{expert}) pair $\pi(d)$ from the joint catalogue. We use a \textbf{calibration argmax with a fusion-default}: the default plan is \emph{fusion(SubspaceAD)}, and we override only when the calibration AUROC of an alternative exceeds the default by at least $3$ pp (the ``override margin''). The 3-pp margin is itself frozen from a one-time sweep on a held-out backbone (GPT-5.4 calibration); we do \emph{not} retune it per backbone. Rationale: $20$ calibration items have a per-domain bootstrap noise floor of $\sim 2$ pp, so a margin slightly above the noise floor avoids over-fitting to small-sample noise.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1440:paper/sections/3_method.tex:50:\paragraph{Why this works.} Three single-strategy regimes map to different per-domain optima: \emph{fusion(SubspaceAD)} dominates texture / few-shot industrial, retail, brain MRI, liver CT, dermoscopy; \emph{direct} wins on semantic domains where the expert distorts the score (LEVIR change, SDNET infrastructure, road obstacle); \emph{expert-only} wins on domains where the expert is near-perfect (GI endoscopy with SubspaceAD AUROC $0.97$). A single fixed strategy cannot capture all three regimes.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1442:paper/sections/3_method.tex:79:\subsection{Refutation agent (v8)}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1444:paper/sections/3_method.tex:82:Beyond the calibration-argmax planner, we introduce a deliberation protocol that directly targets two failure modes we observe in v6.5: (i) \emph{coarse rank grid}---the VLM emits scores drawn from a small set of unique values (11 distinct values on the Qwen3.5-VL test set), which limits ranking resolution and caps AUROC gains; (ii) \emph{confirmation bias}---tool calls tend to support the VLM's working hypothesis rather than test it.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1445:paper/sections/3_method.tex:85:The refutation agent runs on top of the v6 ReAct loop but replaces the free-form ``call any tool, update score'' pattern with a structured three-phase schema:
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1449:paper/sections/3_method.tex:93:In v6.5, a tool was invoked to \emph{support} the VLM's working hypothesis and the system accumulated supporting observations. In v8, every tool output has exactly one legitimate use---retiring a candidate feature---so the protocol is naturally biased toward ruling anomalies \emph{out}. The structured output (\texttt{candidate\_features}, per-turn \texttt{refutation\_verdict}, \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features}) gives a reviewer an auditable trace.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1451:paper/sections/3_method.tex:96:Both v6.5 and v8 standalone sit below Direct on dev (dev macros $0.6942$ and $0.6865$ vs Direct $0.7599$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B). We therefore report the agents after averaging with the Direct VLM score at a single frozen weight selected on dev:
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1453:paper/sections/3_method.tex:100:with $w{=}0.5$ for the v6.5 ensemble and $w{=}0.6$ for the v8 ensemble. Section~\ref{sec:experiments} contains the ablation over $w$. A triple blend $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.1\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5} + 0.4\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ is also reported; all weights are frozen on dev before a single test query is seen.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1455:paper/sections/3_method.tex:103:Section~\ref{sec:experiments} isolates the causal driver of the ensemble gain via rank-preserving and rank-collapsing transformations. Direct emits only $11$ unique scores on our test set, v6.5's free-form JSON protocol emits $49$, and averaging the two fills in the rank ties inside Direct's coarse bimodal clusters with real signal. Transformations that preserve the agent's $49$ unique values---whether keeping the middle-zone mass at $24$\% (the original), inflating it to $100$\% (affine compression), or collapsing it to $0$\% (rank-preserving extreme remap)---all reach the same $+4.7$~pp ensemble gain. A median-split binarisation that collapses the $49$ values to $2$ cuts the gain in half. v8's explicit refutation schema, which assigns scores from a small vocabulary tied to the refutation-verdict decision, retains fewer unique values and therefore captures a smaller share of the ensemble gain. Designing an agent whose deliberation mechanism \emph{also} produces a fine rank grid is the main open direction left by this work.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1459:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:77:\caption{Ablation of agent variants (GPT-5.4, calibration macro AUROC). All use the same descriptor-enhanced v0 as the base.}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1463:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:105:These ablations were run on GPT-5.4 calibration because they represent disqualified designs rather than competitive baselines; we did not re-run them on SeedVL or Qwen3.5 once the failure pattern was clear.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1464:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:110:We benchmarked three expert models on the calibration split (Table~\ref{tab:expert_appendix}). SubspaceAD dominates overall, reaching 1.000 AUROC on D11 VisA calibration where all VLMs score $\leq 0.85$. AnomalyVFM (zero-shot, DINOv2 + LoRA) is uniformly weaker (macro 0.599) and was excluded from AnomalyClaw. DINOv2-PatchNN complements SubspaceAD on D03 infrastructure (0.80 vs 0.50). The oracle that selects the best expert per domain reaches macro 0.898, indicating 7 pp headroom for multi-expert routing as future work.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1465:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:114:\caption{Expert model comparison (calibration, macro AUROC).}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1467:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:133:Table~\ref{tab:descriptor_ablation} shows the per-domain effect of replacing the generic descriptor (``Identify whether the query image is anomalous relative to the reference(s)'') with the task-anchored descriptor (Appendix~\ref{app:benchmark}) on the test split for all three backbones. The macro gain is positive and statistically significant for every backbone (paired bootstrap with 1{,}000 resamples, per-domain stratification, $n{=}1298$): GPT-5.4 $+6.4$ pp [$+4.4, +8.4$], SeedVL $+4.1$ pp [$+1.6, +6.4$], Qwen3.5 $+3.2$ pp [$+1.0, +5.5$]. The three backbones agree on the qualitative direction but disagree on which domains benefit most: GPT-5.4 is dominated by D06 Liver CT and D08 Change ($+31$/$+33$ pp), SeedVL gains most on D08 Change and D09 Road ($+18$/$+14$ pp), Qwen3.5 gains most on D06 Liver and D04 Dermoscopy ($+14$/$+8$ pp). A handful of small per-domain regressions appear on each backbone (e.g.\ D03 Infrastructure on all three, D09 Road on Qwen3.5); these are VLM-strong domains where the more specific prompt trims some true-positive confidence.
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1468:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:137:\caption{Descriptor ablation on the test split: per-domain AUROC (Generic vs Task-anchored, $\Delta$ in pp). Bold = largest per-domain gain per backbone.}
./review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1469:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:143:& \multicolumn{3}{c|}{GPT-5.4} & \multicolumn{3}{c|}{SeedVL} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Qwen3.5} \\
