   158	
   159	Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap} summarises the stratified paired bootstrap for the headline comparisons. The calibration router strictly beats every single-strategy baseline on SeedVL (including fusion, significant); it ties fusion on GPT-5.4 and Qwen3.5 (fusion is the dominant single strategy on those backbones). Against the weaker \emph{direct} baseline that prior agentic-VAD work usually compares to, all three routers---descriptor, calibration, and oracle---show significant gains.
   160	
   161	\subsection{Cross-benchmark generalisation: MMAD}
   162	\label{ssec:mmad}
   163	
   164	We re-evaluated our Qwen3.5-VL-27B agent+Direct ensemble on \textbf{MMAD}~\citep{jiang2024mmad}, a $39{,}670$-question industrial-inspection MCQ benchmark whose Anomaly-Detection subset contains $8{,}297$ Yes/No binary-judgment questions over MVTec-AD, MVTec-LOCO, VisA, and GoodsAD. We report on a stratified $989$-item sample balanced across all $38$ product classes (5--$\sim$50 items per class, seed$=42$). Ground-truth labels are derived from \texttt{Options[Answer]} in \texttt{mmad.json} (preferred) with fallback to the immediate parent folder (\texttt{good}/\texttt{normal}$\,\to\,$label $0$); relying on substring matches against the full path would incorrectly assign label $0$ to every GoodsAD item because ``good'' is contained in ``GoodsAD''. Each query uses the first four \texttt{random\_templates} from \texttt{mmad.json} as normal references (matching MMAD's few-shot$=4$ setup); the Qwen3.5-VL-27B agent is the same unmodified \texttt{agent\_v6.py} used in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, and the ensemble is the same $0.5$-average recipe with no re-tuning.
   165	
   166	\begin{table}[h]
   167	\centering
   168	\caption{\textbf{MMAD cross-benchmark transfer} (Qwen3.5-VL-27B, stratified $n{=}989$ across $38$ classes, Anomaly-Detection subset). We report the MMAD MCQ accuracy (A/B letter derived from the Yes/No option mapping by the score${>}0.5$ rule) and the AUROC that is natively comparable to Table~\ref{tab:main_results}. AUROC gain transfers ($+3.03$ pp pooled, $95\%$ CI $[+1.48,+4.54]$ over $1{,}000$ paired bootstraps, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}0.999$); MCQ accuracy does \emph{not} gain ($-0.8$ pp), consistent with the rank-granularity mechanism of \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity}: averaging two scores changes rank ordering but rarely flips a score's sign relative to $0.5$.}
   169	\label{tab:mmad}
   170	\small
   171	\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
   172	\toprule
   173	 & \multicolumn{3}{c}{MCQ Accuracy (\%)} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Score AUROC} \\
   174	\cmidrule(lr){2-4}\cmidrule(l){5-7}
   175	Subset (\# items)         & Direct & Agent & Ens. & Direct & Agent & Ens. \\
   176	\midrule
   177	DS-MVTec (197)            & 82.2 & 81.2 & 83.2 & 0.914 & 0.890 & 0.930 \\
   178	MVTec-LOCO (187)          & 66.8 & 64.2 & 66.8 & 0.849 & 0.789 & 0.861 \\
   179	VisA (253)                & 67.6 & 70.0 & 67.2 & 0.851 & 0.828 & 0.883 \\
   180	GoodsAD (347)             & 56.8 & 53.3 & 54.2 & 0.628 & 0.582 & 0.665 \\
   181	MVTec-AD (5)              & 40.0 & 40.0 & 40.0 & --- & --- & --- \\
   182	\midrule
   183	\textbf{Pooled} (989)     & \textbf{66.4} & 65.1 & 65.6 & \textbf{0.781} & 0.743 & \textbf{0.811} \\
   184	$\Delta$ Ens $-$ Direct   & --- & --- & $-0.8$ & --- & --- & $\mathbf{+0.030}$ \\
   185	\bottomrule
   186	\end{tabular}
   187	\\[3pt]
   188	{\footnotesize MVTec-AD's $n{=}5$ sub-sample is too small to report stable AUROC. All per-subset AUROC numbers use ground truth derived from \texttt{Options[Answer]}; an earlier version of our evaluator that derived the label by substring match against the full path incorrectly assigned label $0$ to every GoodsAD item (``good'' is contained in ``GoodsAD''), which depressed pooled AUROC numbers by roughly $7$ pp. MCQ Accuracy uses the mapping score${>}0.5 \rightarrow$Yes-letter.}
   189	\end{table}
   190	
   191	Two findings follow. First, the AUROC gain transfers: pooled AUROC moves from $0.781$ (Direct) to $0.811$ (agent$+$Direct ensemble), $+3.03$ pp with $95\%$ CI $[+1.48, +4.54]$ on a paired bootstrap---the same direction and (once labels are derived correctly) a somewhat larger magnitude than Table~\ref{tab:main_results}'s Qwen3.5 test gain. Second, MCQ accuracy does \emph{not} gain ($-0.8$ pp vs Direct). This is consistent with \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity}: averaging scores changes the \emph{rank order} between positive and negative items (helps AUROC) but rarely crosses the decision threshold at $0.5$ (does not help accuracy). A method that needs to improve threshold-based accuracy would need, in addition, a calibrated threshold or a decision rule that exploits the agent's disagreement with Direct near $0.5$; we leave that to future work. The important conclusion is that the ensemble mechanism is benchmark-agnostic at the \emph{ranking} level: on a completely independent benchmark, with different reference-template construction and class taxonomy, it delivers the same AUROC improvement at no inference-time re-tuning.
   192	
   193	\subsection{Extending MMAD beyond AD: a unified task-aware agent (v9)}
   194	\label{ssec:mmad_v9}
   195	
   196	The cross-benchmark experiment of \S\ref{ssec:mmad} only used the
   197	Anomaly-Detection subset (Yes/No). MMAD has eight additional MCQ
   198	types (defect classification / localization / description / analysis,
   199	object classification / analysis / structure / details).  We extend
   200	our agent to a single \emph{unified task-aware} runner (\texttt{agent\_v9.py})
   201	that accepts an optional question and up to four multiple-choice
   202	options, produces (i) a continuous anomaly score (for AD ensembling),
   203	(ii) an MCQ letter, and (iii) a free-text answer in a single forward
   204	pass. Mode is inferred from the inputs in the first cognitive turn;
   205	the 13-tool library is unchanged.
   206	
   207	Using the same Qwen3.5-VL-27B backbone and four normal reference
   208	templates as \S\ref{ssec:mmad}, we re-ran the evaluator on a fresh
   209	stratified $500$-image sample ($n{=}2{,}302$ question-answer pairs
   210	across 38 classes; seed~$42$, option-derived labels).
   211	
   212	\begin{table}[h]
   213	\centering
