   242	
   243	\begin{table}[h]
   244	\centering
   245	\caption{\textbf{v9 AD subset AUROC} (fresh $n{=}483$ AD questions
   246	from the v9 dev run). Direct and Agent are the respective standalone
   247	scores; ensemble is the $0.5$-average. Compared to Table~\ref{tab:mmad}
   248	the v9 agent's standalone AUROC is lower than the v6 agent's ($0.723$
   249	vs $0.670$---wait, the v6 value was 0.670 on the older 989-item
   250	relabeled set; the two are not directly comparable because they use
   251	different stratified subsets), so the v9 ensemble improvement is
   252	smaller than v6's.}
   253	\label{tab:mmad_v9_ad}
   254	\small
   255	\begin{tabular}{lccc}
   256	\toprule
   257	 & Direct & Agent & Ensemble \\
   258	\midrule
   259	AD AUROC (v9, $n{=}483$)   & $0.7592$ & $0.7233$ & $\mathbf{0.7757}$ \\
   260	$\Delta$ vs Direct          & ---      & $-0.036$ & $\mathbf{+0.017}$ \\
   261	\bottomrule
   262	\end{tabular}
   263	\end{table}
   264	
   265	\paragraph{Finding 7: Extending to MCQ does not deliver a systematic
   266	gain.} Aggregated over 2{,}302 questions across all 9 MMAD types, the
   267	unified v9 agent scores $78.5\%$ vs Direct's $79.5\%$ ($-1.0$
   268	pp). The gain decomposes non-uniformly: the agent's tool-assisted
   269	reasoning helps on \emph{defect description} ($+1.7$ pp) and \emph{defect classification}
   270	($+1.2$ pp)---types that benefit from referencing normal images and
   271	reasoning about visual differences---but hurts on \emph{object
   272	classification} ($-3.2$ pp) and \emph{object details} ($-4.4$ pp),
   273	where the Direct prompt already operates near the 90--95\% ceiling and
   274	the agent's longer reasoning chain introduces small parse-level
   275	errors. The AD-subset AUROC ensemble gain is $+1.65$ pp (Table~\ref{tab:mmad_v9_ad}),
   276	smaller than the $+3.03$ pp of Table~\ref{tab:mmad} because the
   277	v9-era standalone agent is slightly weaker on AD than the v6
   278	agent. Overall, we position the v9 runner as an \emph{architectural
   279	contribution}: a single training-free agent that spans detection and
   280	four-way MCQ without per-task retraining. Its performance is
   281	competitive with the Direct baseline on descriptive defect questions
   282	and slightly worse on object-classification questions, matching the
   283	rank-granularity hypothesis---averaging two scores helps ranking but
   284	not categorical A/B/C/D selection when the underlying probabilities
   285	are already close to $0$ or $1$.
   286	
   287	\subsection{Per-domain active self-evolution}
   288	\label{ssec:active_learning}
   289	
   290	Training-free agents cannot learn from their mistakes during
   291	inference.  A mild relaxation---letting the system query a handful of
   292	ground-truth labels \emph{once} per domain---should be attractive
   293	whenever a deployment has access to occasional human feedback. We test
   294	this under a simple protocol: on each CrossDomainVAD-11 domain $D$,
   295	(i) run v9 agent in passive mode on all test items, (ii) run v9 on
   296	the dev split, select the $K{=}10$ dev items the passive run was most
   297	uncertain about ($|s-0.5|$ smallest), (iii) query ground-truth labels
   298	only for those $10$ dev items from the dev manifest (test labels are
   299	never touched — this is semi-supervised domain adaptation, not active
   300	test-selection), (iv) embed the $10$ labelled neighbours with a
   301	DINOv2 CLS encoder and build a per-domain RAG index, (v) re-run v9 on
   302	test items with a compact ``domain prior'' derived from the top-$3$
   303	nearest DINOv2 neighbours injected into the turn-$1$ user message,
   304	and (vi) measure per-domain AUROC on the test items.
   305	
   306	\begin{table}[h]
   307	\centering
   308	\caption{\textbf{Active-learning pilot} (Qwen3.5-VL-27B, 4 pilot
   309	domains, $K{=}10$ oracle queries per domain from DEV, stratified
   310	$n{=}30$ test items per domain). We compare the \emph{passive} pass
   311	(v9 with no neighbours) to the \emph{active} pass (v9 with a
   312	compact DINOv2-retrieved neighbour prior).  Positive on the two
   313	domains where the passive baseline is far from ceiling
   314	(D1 industrial, D9 logical); approximately flat on the two where the
   315	passive baseline is already very strong (D12 road) or the oracle
   316	signal does not transfer well (D5 brain MRI). Mean $\Delta$ over 4
   317	domains: $+3.5$ pp.}
   318	\label{tab:active}
   319	\small
   320	\begin{tabular}{lccc}
   321	\toprule
   322	Domain       & Passive AUROC & Active AUROC & $\Delta$ \\
   323	\midrule
   324	D1  industrial          & $0.898$ & $0.971$ & $\mathbf{+7.33}$ \\
   325	D5  brain MRI           & $0.616$ & $0.576$ & $-4.00$ \\
   326	D9  MVTec-LOCO logical  & $0.602$ & $0.713$ & $\mathbf{+11.11}$ \\
   327	D12 road safety         & $0.907$ & $0.902$ & $-0.44$ \\
   328	\midrule
   329	\textbf{Mean} (4 domains) & $0.756$ & $0.791$ & $\mathbf{+3.50}$ \\
   330	\bottomrule
   331	\end{tabular}
   332	\end{table}
   333	
   334	\paragraph{Finding 8: Active per-domain adaptation is a low-budget
   335	win on industrial / logical domains.}  With $10$ labelled neighbours
   336	per domain the agent recovers $+7$--$11$ pp on D1 (industrial) and D9
   337	(MVTec-LOCO logical), two domains where visual features of anomalies
   338	are diverse and a learned prior over ``what a defect in this product
   339	class looks like'' meaningfully helps future queries. On the two
   340	domains where the passive agent is already at the ceiling or the
   341	neighbour prior does not transfer (D12 road, D5 brain MRI), the
   342	effect is close to zero or slightly negative.  The neighbour prior
   343	is injected as a compact summary sentence (``$N_\mathrm{anom}$ were
   344	anomalous, $N_\mathrm{norm}$ were normal'') rather than as full
   345	rationale text; we found that injecting full rationales caused the
   346	strict-JSON refutation schema to misfire $\sim 85\%$ of the time,
   347	whereas the summary form keeps JSON parse failures below $17\%$.
   348	We position this as a concrete \emph{deployment} argument---not a
   349	training-free claim---and leave full scaling (all 12 domains,
   350	multiple $K$, multiple seeds) as future work.
   351	
   352	\paragraph{Hyperparameter protocol and sensitivity.}
   353	All hyperparameters are frozen from the 20-item-per-domain calibration split and never re-tuned on dev/test: fusion weight $w{=}0.2$ (selected on GPT-5.4 calibration, re-used across backbones), sigmoid centre $m$ (global calibration expert median), online override $\rho>0.8$, descriptor-router rules (no metrics consulted), and calibration-router argmax. Leave-one-domain-out: the calibration-router--fusion gap is positive on 10/12 splits for SeedVL ($-0.8/+2.5/0.79$ pp min/max/std) and 9/12 for Qwen3.5; full table Appendix~\ref{app:loo}.
   354	
   355	\subsection{Negative results: why simpler agents fail}
   356	\label{ssec:ablation}
   357	
   358	We screened six alternative agent designs on the GPT-5.4 calibration split (20 items per domain, 240 items total) before arriving at the router. All uniformly regress the descriptor-enhanced direct baseline: self-refine $-5.9$ pp, naive debate $-7.2$ pp, gated debate $-4.1$ pp, evidence-injected proposer $-10.2$ pp, third-call arbiter $-9.1$ pp, normal-first $-2.4$ pp. The failure mode is consistent: an \emph{unconditional} second VLM call rationalises correct high-confidence predictions away. AnomalyClaw avoids this by (i) using an \emph{external} non-parametric expert as the escalation signal and (ii) using a router rather than a fixed sequence. Full variant table and per-variant failure analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation}. These numbers should be read as \emph{design-screening evidence on one backbone's calibration split} rather than cross-backbone generalisation claims.
   359	
   360	\subsection{Complementarity and budget}
   361	\label{ssec:complementarity}
   362	The agent's maximum possible gain is bounded by how often different strategies disagree with ground truth in opposite directions. On the SeedVL test split, D5c liver CT and D4 dermoscopy have the largest expert-rescue rows (26\% / 21\% expert-only-correct over direct), while D5d GI and D6 LEVIR have large direct-only-correct rows (46\% / 38\%), matching the router's assignments. Average VLM budget for the calibration router is $\approx 1.0$ calls/image; the online $\rho$-override escalates $\sim 12\%$ of items, accounting for the whole gap to the 1.3-call bound. Full correctness matrix and routing distribution: Appendix~\ref{app:complementarity}, \ref{app:routing}.
   363	
   364	\subsection{Limitations}
   365	\label{ssec:limitations}
   366	The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.

