review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2347:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:24:\caption{\textbf{Main results} (test split, 12 domain codes, $n{=}1418$). Macro-averaged AUROC across domains. \textbf{Bold} = best per backbone excluding Oracle. Our method is the \emph{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} (a per-item autonomous ReAct agent with 13 tools, K=5 turn budget, no per-domain hardcoding) composed with Direct VLM via post-hoc averaging (\texttt{compose\_ensemble.py}, $\alpha{=}0.5$).}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2352:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:81:Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). On GPT-5.4 dev the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2359:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:165:The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2387:paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:3:\textbf{Main finding.} On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items), a simple arithmetic ensemble of the VLM's Direct score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline $0.7684$ by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The ensemble recipe is $s_{\mathrm{final}} = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, \emph{frozen} on dev before any test query is seen. The recipe beats the stronger SubspaceAD \emph{fusion} baseline by $+0.9$ pp on GPT-5.4 and matches it on Qwen3.5, using only VLM outputs, no additional expert model.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2392:paper/sections/3_method.tex:30:    \item[\textbf{AnomalyVFM}~\citep{anomalyvfm2025}.] Vision foundation model adapted via LoRA for zero-shot anomaly segmentation, with a convolutional decoder. We use the DINOv2 backbone variant; provides image-level score plus pixel mask. Complementary to SubspaceAD on infrastructure (calibration AUROC $0.72$ on D4 vs SubspaceAD's $0.50$) and on MVTec-LOCO logical anomalies where its synthetic-anomaly training data covers compositional defects.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2515:Vision-language models (VLMs) have become the default tool for training-free visual anomaly detection (VAD): a practitioner uploads a query image, optionally attaches one or two normal reference images, and asks the model whether the query is abnormal. This recipe drives much of the recent published progress on industrial~\citep{gu2024anomalygpt,jeong2023winclip}, medical~\citep{bao2024bmad}, and cross-domain~\citep{jiang2024mmad} benchmarks. The appeal is obvious: no training, no architecture changes, and a single VLM call per image.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2525:Two observations drive this work. First, a cross-domain VAD deployment must handle textural industrial defects, semantic road obstacles, brain-MRI lesions, and infrastructure cracks with the same pipeline. The same fixed recipe---no matter how clever---cannot be optimal on all of them simultaneously: we find that on our 12-domain test split, the argmax strategy varies by domain family and gains of up to $+3$ pp macro AUROC are available simply by switching strategy per domain (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a). Second, recent ``agentic VAD'' systems~\citep{zhang2025agentiad,chen2026eagle,wei2025autoiad} bundle three decisions---what the VLM attends to, which expert provides evidence, and how to aggregate---into a single monolithic pipeline that is hard to ablate and hard to compare against simpler baselines.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2695:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:77:\caption{Ablation of agent variants (GPT-5.4, calibration macro AUROC). All use the same descriptor-enhanced v0 as the base.}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2697:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:110:We benchmarked three expert models on the calibration split (Table~\ref{tab:expert_appendix}). SubspaceAD dominates overall, reaching 1.000 AUROC on D11 VisA calibration where all VLMs score $\leq 0.85$. AnomalyVFM (zero-shot, DINOv2 + LoRA) is uniformly weaker (macro 0.599) and was excluded from AnomalyClaw. DINOv2-PatchNN complements SubspaceAD on D03 infrastructure (0.80 vs 0.50). The oracle that selects the best expert per domain reaches macro 0.898, indicating 7 pp headroom for multi-expert routing as future work.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2698:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:114:\caption{Expert model comparison (calibration, macro AUROC).}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2699:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:174:\caption{Per-domain AUROC per strategy (all three backbones). Red $\blacktriangledown$ marks the strategy selected by the calibration router for that (backbone, domain) cell.}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2700:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:209:\caption{Leave-one-domain-out macro AUROC gain of score fusion over v0. All 11 LOO splits are positive on every backbone.}
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2721:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:310:On D10 calibration (20 items, 4 reference images per query), Route C reaches 60\% accuracy versus v0's 55\%. The gain is modest for two reasons: (i) VLMs struggle to count small similar objects reliably, and (ii) the 20-item calibration split yields noisy threshold estimates. Route C is \emph{not} part of the main-table AnomalyClaw variant reported in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: the reported numbers use only Routes A/B/D. We include Route C here as a concrete direction for future work and flag it as a prototype rather than a validated component of the agent.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2724:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:330:The descriptor router maps each domain family to a default strategy. Rules were frozen once from the benchmark descriptor before any test query; no calibration AUROC was consulted.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2734:paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:14:Two observations drive this work. First, a cross-domain VAD deployment must handle textural industrial defects, semantic road obstacles, brain-MRI lesions, and infrastructure cracks with the same pipeline. The same fixed recipe---no matter how clever---cannot be optimal on all of them simultaneously: we find that on our 12-domain test split, the argmax strategy varies by domain family and gains of up to $+3$ pp macro AUROC are available simply by switching strategy per domain (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a). Second, recent ``agentic VAD'' systems~\citep{zhang2025agentiad,chen2026eagle,wei2025autoiad} bundle three decisions---what the VLM attends to, which expert provides evidence, and how to aggregate---into a single monolithic pipeline that is hard to ablate and hard to compare against simpler baselines.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2744:I’m going to run one independent reviewer pass in parallel now, using the same repo evidence, while I do a tighter local diagnostic on the ablation confound. The key question is whether “middle mass” remains causal when the transformation preserves AUROC/rank exactly.
