review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:546:   AND capture v6.5's score diversity. Hypothesis: v9+Direct > v6.5+Direct.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:785:Vision-language models (VLMs) have become the default tool for training-free visual anomaly detection (VAD), yet single-pass prompting fails silently on domains where the VLM's world-knowledge priors conflict with the task-specific anomaly definition. We study how to add structure on top of a VLM for \emph{cross-domain} VAD and identify two coupled failure modes in modern VAD agents: (i) \emph{output bimodality}---VLMs emit extreme scores (0.0x or 0.9x) with almost no middle-zone mass, which caps ranking accuracy; (ii) \emph{confirmation bias}---once the VLM commits to an anomaly hypothesis, tool calls tend to accumulate supporting evidence rather than test it.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:789:On our \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} benchmark (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items, Qwen3.5-VL-27B backbone), a simple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5\text{-}agent}$ ensemble reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline ($0.7684$) by \textbf{$+4.52$ pp} (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta>0){=}1.000$ on $1{,}418$ items). The refutation agent (v8) ensembled with Direct reaches $0.7812$ ($+1.28$ pp) and provides interpretable traces; a triple blend $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.1\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5\text{-}agent} + 0.4\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ sits in between at $0.8036$ ($+3.52$ pp). On GPT-5.4 dev the same recipe generalises: the best triple blend over Direct, v6.6 self-ensemble, and v8 gives $+2.4$ pp (n=480). We localise the source of ensemble gain with score-distribution histograms: the $+4.5$ pp effect correlates with the agent's middle-zone mass ($24$\% for v6.5 vs $0.4$\% for v8), not with the agent's stand-alone accuracy.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:825:    \item[\textbf{AnomalyVFM}~\citep{anomalyvfm2025}.] Vision foundation model adapted via LoRA for zero-shot anomaly segmentation, with a convolutional decoder. We use the DINOv2 backbone variant; provides image-level score plus pixel mask. Complementary to SubspaceAD on infrastructure (calibration AUROC $0.72$ on D4 vs SubspaceAD's $0.50$) and on MVTec-LOCO logical anomalies where its synthetic-anomaly training data covers compositional defects.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:834:    \item[\textbf{expert-only}.] No VLM call; the expert score is sigmoid-rescaled around the calibration median and used directly. Selected for domains where the calibration AUROC of the expert exceeds the best VLM-based strategy by $\ge 3$ pp (e.g.\ D7 GI endoscopy with SubspaceAD).
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:841:For each domain $d$, the agent selects a (\emph{strategy}, \emph{expert}) pair $\pi(d)$ from the joint catalogue. We use a \textbf{calibration argmax with a fusion-default}: the default plan is \emph{fusion(SubspaceAD)}, and we override only when the calibration AUROC of an alternative exceeds the default by at least $3$ pp (the ``override margin''). The 3-pp margin is itself frozen from a one-time sweep on a held-out backbone (GPT-5.4 calibration); we do \emph{not} retune it per backbone. Rationale: $20$ calibration items have a per-domain bootstrap noise floor of $\sim 2$ pp, so a margin slightly above the noise floor avoids over-fitting to small-sample noise.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:845:\paragraph{Why this works.} Three single-strategy regimes map to different per-domain optima: \emph{fusion(SubspaceAD)} dominates texture / few-shot industrial, retail, brain MRI, liver CT, dermoscopy; \emph{direct} wins on semantic domains where the expert distorts the score (LEVIR change, SDNET infrastructure, road obstacle); \emph{expert-only} wins on domains where the expert is near-perfect (GI endoscopy with SubspaceAD AUROC $0.97$). A single fixed strategy cannot capture all three regimes.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:930:Per-domain AUROC; macro-averaged across the 12 domain codes present in test. All thresholds and router assignments frozen from calibration. VLM anomaly scores extracted via structured JSON output (schema in Appendix~\ref{app:scoring}); $s = c$ if label is ``anomalous'' else $1 - c$. Bootstrap 95\% CIs via stratified paired bootstrap (per-domain stratification, $1{,}000$ resamples).
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:937:\caption{\textbf{Main results} (test split, 12 domain codes, $n{=}1418$). Macro-averaged AUROC across domains. \textbf{Bold} = best per backbone excluding Oracle. Our method is the \emph{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} (a per-item autonomous ReAct agent with 13 tools, K=5 turn budget, no per-domain hardcoding) composed with Direct VLM via post-hoc averaging (\texttt{compose\_ensemble.py}, $\alpha{=}0.5$).}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:978:Replacing a generic domain descriptor with a task-anchored one significantly improves all three backbones at zero inference cost (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a): macro AUROC moves from 0.761 to 0.825 on GPT-5.4 ($+6.4$ pp, 95\% CI $[+4.4,+8.4]$), 0.748 to 0.789 on SeedVL ($+4.1$ pp, $[+1.6,+6.4]$), and 0.760 to 0.792 on Qwen3.5 ($+3.2$ pp, $[+1.0,+5.5]$). The gain concentrates in domains where VLM world-knowledge priors conflict with the task-specific anomaly definition (D08 LEVIR change, D06 BMAD liver CT); full per-backbone per-domain evidence is in Appendix~\ref{app:descriptor}. We recommend this as baseline practice for any training-free VAD system.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:994:Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). On GPT-5.4 dev the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:1017:The calibration router's choice coincides with the oracle on 9/12 domains for GPT-5.4 and 10/12 for both SeedVL and Qwen3.5. Mismatches concentrate where two strategies are within 1 pp on calibration (a bootstrap-level tie that the router cannot reliably resolve with 20 items), and the realised AUROC gap on test is small ($\le 0.06$). The per-backbone per-strategy per-domain table and the per-domain router-choice overlay are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain} (Figure~\ref{fig:per_domain_appendix}).
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:1024:\caption{Stratified paired bootstrap summary (test split, $1{,}000$ resamples, per-domain stratification). $\Delta$ is the pairwise macro-AUROC gap; CI is the 95\% percentile interval; \checkmark\ means the CI excludes zero.}
review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:1065:The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.
