review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:2933:/bin/bash -lc 'rg -n "4x_mmad|mmad_fulltype|ssec:mmad|include|input" paper/main.tex paper/sections/4_experiments.tex' in /hdd1/jiangxi/AD-Agent
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:2935:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:162:\label{ssec:mmad}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:2960:   161	\subsection{Cross-benchmark generalisation: MMAD}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:2961:   162	\label{ssec:mmad}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:2963:   164	We re-evaluated our Qwen3.5-VL-27B agent+Direct ensemble on \textbf{MMAD}~\citep{jiang2024mmad}, a $39{,}670$-question industrial-inspection MCQ benchmark whose Anomaly-Detection subset contains $8{,}297$ Yes/No binary-judgment questions over MVTec-AD, MVTec-LOCO, VisA, and GoodsAD. We report on a stratified $989$-item sample balanced across all $38$ product classes (5--$\sim$50 items per class, seed$=42$). Each query uses the first four \texttt{random\_templates} from \texttt{mmad.json} as normal references (matching MMAD's few-shot$=4$ setup); the Qwen3.5-VL-27B agent is the same unmodified \texttt{agent\_v6.py} used in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, and the ensemble is the same $0.5$-average recipe with no re-tuning.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:2967:   168	\caption{\textbf{MMAD cross-benchmark transfer} (Qwen3.5-VL-27B, stratified $n{=}989$ across $38$ classes, Anomaly-Detection subset). We report both the MMAD MCQ accuracy (A=Yes/B=No answer from score${>}0.5$) and the AUROC that is natively comparable to Table~\ref{tab:main_results}. AUROC gain transfers ($+2.31$ pp pooled, $95\%$ CI $[+0.8,+3.9]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}0.996$); MCQ accuracy does \emph{not} gain, consistent with the rank-granularity mechanism of \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity}: averaging two scores changes rank ordering but rarely flips a score's sign relative to $0.5$.}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:2968:   169	\label{tab:mmad}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:2972:   173	 & \multicolumn{3}{c}{MCQ Accuracy (\%)} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Score AUROC} \\
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:2987:   188	{\footnotesize GoodsAD and MVTec-AD do not report AUROC because the stratified $989$-item sample has only one label class present for these subsets (GoodsAD: all 347 items label$=$1 because the subset has no dedicated ``good'' folder; MVTec-AD: $n{=}5$ sample is too small). The subset-level accuracy is reported without AUROC. MCQ Accuracy uses the mapping score${>}0.5 \rightarrow$A, else B.}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:2990:   191	Two findings follow. First, the AUROC gain transfers: pooled AUROC moves from $0.708$ (Direct) to $0.731$ (agent$+$Direct ensemble), $+2.31$ pp with $95\%$ CI $[+0.8, +3.9]$ on a paired bootstrap---the same direction and comparable magnitude as Table~\ref{tab:main_results}'s Qwen3.5 test gain. Second, MCQ accuracy does \emph{not} gain ($-0.8$ pp vs Direct). This is consistent with \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity}: averaging scores changes the \emph{rank order} between positive and negative items (helps AUROC) but rarely crosses the decision threshold at $0.5$ (does not help accuracy). A method that needs to improve threshold-based accuracy would need, in addition, a calibrated threshold or a decision rule that exploits the agent's disagreement with Direct near $0.5$; we leave that to future work. The important conclusion is that the ensemble mechanism is benchmark-agnostic at the \emph{ranking} level: on a completely independent benchmark, with different reference-template construction and class taxonomy, it delivers the same AUROC improvement at no inference-time re-tuning.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:3006:   207	The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.
