refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3711:`auroc_tool`, `auroc_direct`, and `delta` use macro AUROC when available, but the bootstrap CI uses pooled AUROC over resampled items. So a card can claim “macro Δ has CI lower > 0” when the CI is actually for pooled Δ.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3713:Exact fix: bootstrap the same metric you report. Resample paired items within domain, recompute per-domain AUROC, then average domains. If a slice falls back to pooled AUROC, label it explicitly as pooled and use pooled for both point estimate and CI.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3801:1. Run a two-stage dev protocol: use dev-A to discover niches, dev-B to validate them with corrected macro-AUROC CIs, then freeze and run test once.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3832:`auroc_tool`, `auroc_direct`, and `delta` use macro AUROC when available, but the bootstrap CI uses pooled AUROC over resampled items. So a card can claim “macro Δ has CI lower > 0” when the CI is actually for pooled Δ.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3834:Exact fix: bootstrap the same metric you report. Resample paired items within domain, recompute per-domain AUROC, then average domains. If a slice falls back to pooled AUROC, label it explicitly as pooled and use pooled for both point estimate and CI.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3922:1. Run a two-stage dev protocol: use dev-A to discover niches, dev-B to validate them with corrected macro-AUROC CIs, then freeze and run test once.
