review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:498:\textbf{Main finding.} On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items), a simple arithmetic ensemble of the VLM's Direct score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline $0.7684$ by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The ensemble recipe is $s_{\mathrm{final}} = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, \emph{frozen} on dev before any test query is seen. The recipe beats the stronger SubspaceAD \emph{fusion} baseline by $+0.9$ pp on GPT-5.4 and matches it on Qwen3.5, using only VLM outputs, no additional expert model.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:525:Per-domain AUROC; macro-averaged across the 12 domain codes present in test. All thresholds and router assignments frozen from calibration. VLM anomaly scores extracted via structured JSON output (schema in Appendix~\ref{app:scoring}); $s = c$ if label is ``anomalous'' else $1 - c$. Bootstrap 95\% CIs via stratified paired bootstrap (per-domain stratification, $1{,}000$ resamples).
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:532:\caption{\textbf{Main results} (test split, 12 domain codes, $n{=}1418$). Macro-averaged AUROC across domains. \textbf{Bold} = best per backbone excluding Oracle. Our method is the \emph{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} (a per-item autonomous ReAct agent with 13 tools, K=5 turn budget, no per-domain hardcoding) composed with Direct VLM via post-hoc averaging (\texttt{compose\_ensemble.py}, $\alpha{=}0.5$).}
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:573:Replacing a generic domain descriptor with a task-anchored one significantly improves all three backbones at zero inference cost (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a): macro AUROC moves from 0.761 to 0.825 on GPT-5.4 ($+6.4$ pp, 95\% CI $[+4.4,+8.4]$), 0.748 to 0.789 on SeedVL ($+4.1$ pp, $[+1.6,+6.4]$), and 0.760 to 0.792 on Qwen3.5 ($+3.2$ pp, $[+1.0,+5.5]$). The gain concentrates in domains where VLM world-knowledge priors conflict with the task-specific anomaly definition (D08 LEVIR change, D06 BMAD liver CT); full per-backbone per-domain evidence is in Appendix~\ref{app:descriptor}. We recommend this as baseline practice for any training-free VAD system.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:589:Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). On GPT-5.4 dev the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:598:    \item \textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}: monotone-remap v6 scores to linearly spaced points in $[0.05, 0.19]\cup[0.81, 0.95]$, preserving every unique rank but moving all 49 values outside $[0.2, 0.8]$. Preserves rank granularity AND standalone AUROC; destroys middle-zone mass.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:606:\caption{Controlled ablation on Qwen3.5-VL-27B test ($n{=}1418$, macro AUROC over 12 domains). Columns: the agent variant, its standalone AUROC, the $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}+0.5\!\cdot\!X$ ensemble AUROC, the \emph{number of unique score values}, the middle-zone mass, and the Spearman correlation with Direct. The key row is \textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}: middle-mass drops to $0\%$ but rank granularity is preserved, and the ensemble gain actually \emph{increases} to $+4.68$ pp. Conversely \textbf{v6-BIN} keeps sign-level direction but collapses ranks from $49$ to $2$, and the ensemble drops to $+2.31$ pp. Middle-mass is correlational with rank granularity on unprocessed outputs; rank granularity is what causes ensemble gain.}
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:632:The calibration router's choice coincides with the oracle on 9/12 domains for GPT-5.4 and 10/12 for both SeedVL and Qwen3.5. Mismatches concentrate where two strategies are within 1 pp on calibration (a bootstrap-level tie that the router cannot reliably resolve with 20 items), and the realised AUROC gap on test is small ($\le 0.06$). The per-backbone per-strategy per-domain table and the per-domain router-choice overlay are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain} (Figure~\ref{fig:per_domain_appendix}).
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:639:\caption{Stratified paired bootstrap summary (test split, $1{,}000$ resamples, per-domain stratification). $\Delta$ is the pairwise macro-AUROC gap; CI is the 95\% percentile interval; \checkmark\ means the CI excludes zero.}
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:680:The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.
