review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:2107:review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2352:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:81:Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). On GPT-5.4 dev the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:2126:review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2387:paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:3:\textbf{Main finding.} On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items), a simple arithmetic ensemble of the VLM's Direct score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline $0.7684$ by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The ensemble recipe is $s_{\mathrm{final}} = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, \emph{frozen} on dev before any test query is seen. The recipe beats the stronger SubspaceAD \emph{fusion} baseline by $+0.9$ pp on GPT-5.4 and matches it on Qwen3.5, using only VLM outputs, no additional expert model.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:2145:review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2744:I’m going to run one independent reviewer pass in parallel now, using the same repo evidence, while I do a tighter local diagnostic on the ablation confound. The key question is whether “middle mass” remains causal when the transformation preserves AUROC/rank exactly.
