review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:4401:review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:546:   AND capture v6.5's score diversity. Hypothesis: v9+Direct > v6.5+Direct.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:4463:review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:785:Vision-language models (VLMs) have become the default tool for training-free visual anomaly detection (VAD), yet single-pass prompting fails silently on domains where the VLM's world-knowledge priors conflict with the task-specific anomaly definition. We study how to add structure on top of a VLM for \emph{cross-domain} VAD and identify two coupled failure modes in modern VAD agents: (i) \emph{output bimodality}---VLMs emit extreme scores (0.0x or 0.9x) with almost no middle-zone mass, which caps ranking accuracy; (ii) \emph{confirmation bias}---once the VLM commits to an anomaly hypothesis, tool calls tend to accumulate supporting evidence rather than test it.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:4465:review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:789:On our \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} benchmark (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items, Qwen3.5-VL-27B backbone), a simple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5\text{-}agent}$ ensemble reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline ($0.7684$) by \textbf{$+4.52$ pp} (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta>0){=}1.000$ on $1{,}418$ items). The refutation agent (v8) ensembled with Direct reaches $0.7812$ ($+1.28$ pp) and provides interpretable traces; a triple blend $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.1\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5\text{-}agent} + 0.4\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ sits in between at $0.8036$ ($+3.52$ pp). On GPT-5.4 dev the same recipe generalises: the best triple blend over Direct, v6.6 self-ensemble, and v8 gives $+2.4$ pp (n=480). We localise the source of ensemble gain with score-distribution histograms: the $+4.5$ pp effect correlates with the agent's middle-zone mass ($24$\% for v6.5 vs $0.4$\% for v8), not with the agent's stand-alone accuracy.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:4484:review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:937:\caption{\textbf{Main results} (test split, 12 domain codes, $n{=}1418$). Macro-averaged AUROC across domains. \textbf{Bold} = best per backbone excluding Oracle. Our method is the \emph{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} (a per-item autonomous ReAct agent with 13 tools, K=5 turn budget, no per-domain hardcoding) composed with Direct VLM via post-hoc averaging (\texttt{compose\_ensemble.py}, $\alpha{=}0.5$).}
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:4497:review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:994:Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). On GPT-5.4 dev the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
