review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:853:./AnomaClaw/LIT_SURVEY.md:70:The IAD field is undergoing a paradigm shift from traditional unsupervised methods (PatchCore, SPADE, RD4AD) toward MLLM-based reasoning. However, this shift has created a bifurcation: **reasoning-focused papers** optimize for MMAD benchmark accuracy, while **detection-focused papers** optimize for MVTec/VisA AUROC. Neither addresses real deployment scenarios where:
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:856:./paper/sections/2_related_work.tex:8:Recent work uses large VLMs directly for anomaly decisions. AnomalyGPT~\citep{gu2024anomalygpt} fine-tunes a VLM on industrial defect data with simulated anomalies; MMAD~\citep{jiang2024mmad} evaluates several frontier VLMs on a multi-task anomaly benchmark. The dominant deployment pattern, however, remains a \emph{single forward pass} with a short prompt asking whether the image is abnormal, optionally with one or two reference images. As we show in \S\ref{sec:experiments}, this pattern is substantially under-calibrated when paired with generic domain descriptions: a one-sentence task-anchored descriptor alone moves a frontier VLM from $0.761$ to $0.825$ macro AUROC. AnomalyClaw stays on top of such a VLM without any fine-tuning and treats the descriptor-enhanced single-pass call as its first phase.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:858:./paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:4:We introduced \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free visual anomaly detection framework whose headline method is score-diverse VLM-agent ensembling: a simple arithmetic average of a Direct VLM score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score. On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} ($1{,}418$ test items, 12 domains) the ensemble reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating descriptor-only Direct by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). On GPT-5.4 it reaches $0.864$, edging the SubspaceAD-fusion baseline by $+0.9$ pp while using no external expert model. We localise the mechanism to \emph{score-rank granularity}: Direct emits $11$ unique scores on the test set, the agent emits $49$; averaging breaks ties inside Direct's coarse bimodal clusters with the agent's finer rank information. Controlled rank-preserving transformations confirm this: $0\%$ middle-mass with $49$ unique values still delivers $+4.68$ pp, whereas $0\%$ middle-mass with only $2$ unique values delivers $+2.31$ pp.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:861:./paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:11:The v6 ensemble's macro-AUROC gain over SubspaceAD fusion is positive only on GPT-5.4 ($+0.9$ pp) and is tied on Qwen3.5 ($\pm 0$ pp). The refutation agent's Qwen3.5 test gain is positive-bootstrap but its CI touches zero; its stored test JSON was produced before a minor schema fix (corrected code is released; re-run not executed). GPT-5.4 v8 test at scale repeatedly rate-limits our proprietary API gateway, so GPT-5.4 numbers for v8 are dev-only ($n{=}480$). The v8 ensemble claim therefore generalises across backbones only at dev-split resolution, not test. The agent+Direct recipe matches the SubspaceAD-fusion baseline on Qwen3.5 rather than strictly outperforming it; we report it as a VLM-only alternative that avoids a domain-specialised expert.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:863:./paper/sections/4x_mmad_fulltype.tex:2:% Will be spliced into 4_experiments.tex after \subsection{Cross-benchmark generalisation: MMAD}.
