review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:1107:./paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:14:Two observations drive this work. First, a cross-domain VAD deployment must handle textural industrial defects, semantic road obstacles, brain-MRI lesions, and infrastructure cracks with the same pipeline. The same fixed recipe---no matter how clever---cannot be optimal on all of them simultaneously: we find that on our 12-domain test split, the argmax strategy varies by domain family and gains of up to $+3$ pp macro AUROC are available simply by switching strategy per domain (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a). Second, recent ``agentic VAD'' systems~\citep{zhang2025agentiad,chen2026eagle,wei2025autoiad} bundle three decisions---what the VLM attends to, which expert provides evidence, and how to aggregate---into a single monolithic pipeline that is hard to ablate and hard to compare against simpler baselines.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:1108:./paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:28:    \item \textbf{Score-diverse VLM-agent ensembling is the headline method.} A simple arithmetic average $s_{\mathrm{final}}{=}0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}}+0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$ reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B test ($n{=}1{,}418$, 12 domains), beating descriptor-only Direct by $+4.53$~pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The recipe matches SubspaceAD fusion on Qwen3.5 and edges it by $+0.9$~pp on GPT-5.4, using only VLM outputs.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:1112:./research-wiki/query_pack.md:7:AnomalyClaw: agent-based industrial anomaly detection using adversarial debate between Proposer/Advocate VLM agents, expert evidence (PatchCore, DINOv2 retrieval), and multi-reference calibration. Evaluated on MMAD 12-domain benchmark. Target: surpass Cal-Tuned Fusion (0.8819 macro AUROC).
