review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6703:    17	Per-domain AUROC; macro-averaged across the 12 domain codes present in test. All thresholds and router assignments frozen from calibration. VLM anomaly scores extracted via structured JSON output (schema in Appendix~\ref{app:scoring}); $s = c$ if label is ``anomalous'' else $1 - c$. Bootstrap 95\% CIs via stratified paired bootstrap (per-domain stratification, $1{,}000$ resamples).
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6710:    24	\caption{\textbf{Main results: per-domain AUROC across three VLM backbones} (test split, $n{=}1{,}418$). For each backbone we report the descriptor-only Direct VLM and our \emph{agent+Direct ensemble} ($s = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, weights frozen on dev). Agent choice per backbone: v6.5 on Qwen3.5/SeedVL, v6.6 self-ensemble on GPT-5.4 (selected on dev). \textbf{Macro} row at the bottom; $\Delta$ is ensemble macro minus Direct macro. 95\% paired bootstrap CIs on macro $\Delta$ are reported under the table; the gain is significant at $95\%$ on GPT-5.4 and Qwen3.5 ($P(\Delta\!>\!0)\!\ge\!1.000$), positive but not significant on SeedVL ($P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}0.927$).}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6741:    55	For reference, the descriptor-only Direct VLM and the SubspaceAD-fusion baseline (single call; $s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.2\,\sigma(s_\mathrm{SubspaceAD})$) have macro AUROC $0.855/0.808/0.814$ on GPT-5.4/SeedVL/Qwen3.5; the agent+Direct ensemble reaches $0.864/0.809/0.814$: $+0.87/+0.03/\pm 0$\,pp vs the expert-assisted fusion baseline, using \emph{no} external expert model. All backbones use the same \texttt{v6\_direct\_*} descriptor Direct (single canonical file per backbone; see Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}); an earlier SeedVL draft used a pre-v6 descriptor run (\texttt{seedvl\_v0\_direct\_test\_all\_v2.json}) which gave a systematically weaker Direct baseline ($0.7794$) and correspondingly a larger $\Delta$ ($+2.14$ pp); we rectify the provenance here. Per-backbone single-system agent results (v6.5 or v6.6 alone, no ensemble), v8 refutation ensemble, and the triple blend are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}.}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6754:    68	Replacing a generic domain descriptor with a task-anchored one significantly improves all three backbones at zero inference cost (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a): macro AUROC moves from 0.761 to 0.825 on GPT-5.4 ($+6.4$ pp, 95\% CI $[+4.4,+8.4]$), 0.748 to 0.789 on SeedVL ($+4.1$ pp, $[+1.6,+6.4]$), and 0.760 to 0.792 on Qwen3.5 ($+3.2$ pp, $[+1.0,+5.5]$). The gain concentrates in domains where VLM world-knowledge priors conflict with the task-specific anomaly definition (D08 LEVIR change, D06 BMAD liver CT); full per-backbone per-domain evidence is in Appendix~\ref{app:descriptor}. We recommend this as baseline practice for any training-free VAD system.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6770:    84	Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$; one-sided bootstrap probability $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). Because the CI touches zero, we characterise this gain as positive-bootstrap rather than strictly significant. On GPT-5.4 dev (no test due to proprietary API rate-limit issues for concurrent v8 runs), the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). A minor schema fix was applied to the v8 runner after this test-set inference was completed; the reported v8 AUROC therefore reflects the pre-fix run. The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6779:    93	    \item \textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}: monotone-remap v6 scores to linearly spaced points in $[0.05, 0.19]\cup[0.81, 0.95]$, preserving every unique rank but moving all 49 values outside $[0.2, 0.8]$. Preserves rank granularity AND standalone AUROC; destroys middle-zone mass.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6787:   101	\caption{Controlled ablation on Qwen3.5-VL-27B test ($n{=}1418$, macro AUROC over 12 domains). Columns: the agent variant, its standalone AUROC, the $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}+0.5\!\cdot\!X$ ensemble AUROC, the \emph{number of unique score values}, the middle-zone mass, and the Spearman correlation with Direct. The key row is \textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}: middle-mass drops to $0\%$ but rank granularity is preserved, and the ensemble gain actually \emph{increases} to $+4.68$ pp. Conversely \textbf{v6-BIN} keeps sign-level direction but collapses ranks from $49$ to $2$, and the ensemble drops to $+2.31$ pp. Middle-mass is correlational with rank granularity on unprocessed outputs; rank granularity is what causes ensemble gain.}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6813:   127	The calibration router's choice coincides with the oracle on 9/12 domains for GPT-5.4 and 10/12 for both SeedVL and Qwen3.5. Mismatches concentrate where two strategies are within 1 pp on calibration (a bootstrap-level tie that the router cannot reliably resolve with 20 items), and the realised AUROC gap on test is small ($\le 0.06$). The per-backbone per-strategy per-domain table and the per-domain router-choice overlay are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain} (Figure~\ref{fig:per_domain_appendix}).
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6820:   134	\caption{Stratified paired bootstrap summary (test split, $1{,}000$ resamples, per-domain stratification). $\Delta$ is the pairwise macro-AUROC gap; CI is the 95\% percentile interval; \checkmark\ means the CI excludes zero.}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6847:   161	\subsection{Cross-benchmark generalisation: MMAD}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6848:   162	\label{ssec:mmad}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6850:   164	We re-evaluated our Qwen3.5-VL-27B agent+Direct ensemble on \textbf{MMAD}~\citep{jiang2024mmad}, a $39{,}670$-question industrial-inspection MCQ benchmark whose Anomaly-Detection subset contains $8{,}297$ Yes/No binary-judgment questions over MVTec-AD, MVTec-LOCO, VisA, and GoodsAD. We report on a stratified $989$-item sample balanced across all $38$ product classes (5--$\sim$50 items per class, seed$=42$). Ground-truth labels are derived from \texttt{Options[Answer]} in \texttt{mmad.json} (preferred) with fallback to the immediate parent folder (\texttt{good}/\texttt{normal}$\,\to\,$label $0$); relying on substring matches against the full path would incorrectly assign label $0$ to every GoodsAD item because ``good'' is contained in ``GoodsAD''. Each query uses the first four \texttt{random\_templates} from \texttt{mmad.json} as normal references (matching MMAD's few-shot$=4$ setup); the Qwen3.5-VL-27B agent is the same unmodified \texttt{agent\_v6.py} used in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, and the ensemble is the same $0.5$-average recipe with no re-tuning.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6854:   168	\caption{\textbf{MMAD cross-benchmark transfer} (Qwen3.5-VL-27B, stratified $n{=}989$ across $38$ classes, Anomaly-Detection subset). We report the MMAD MCQ accuracy (A/B letter derived from the Yes/No option mapping by the score${>}0.5$ rule) and the AUROC that is natively comparable to Table~\ref{tab:main_results}. AUROC gain transfers ($+3.03$ pp pooled, $95\%$ CI $[+1.48,+4.54]$ over $1{,}000$ paired bootstraps, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}0.999$); MCQ accuracy does \emph{not} gain ($-0.8$ pp), consistent with the rank-granularity mechanism of \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity}: averaging two scores changes rank ordering but rarely flips a score's sign relative to $0.5$.}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6855:   169	\label{tab:mmad}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6859:   173	 & \multicolumn{3}{c}{MCQ Accuracy (\%)} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Score AUROC} \\
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6874:   188	{\footnotesize MVTec-AD's $n{=}5$ sub-sample is too small to report stable AUROC. All per-subset AUROC numbers use ground truth derived from \texttt{Options[Answer]}; an earlier version of our evaluator that derived the label by substring match against the full path incorrectly assigned label $0$ to every GoodsAD item (``good'' is contained in ``GoodsAD''), which depressed pooled AUROC numbers by roughly $7$ pp. MCQ Accuracy uses the mapping score${>}0.5 \rightarrow$Yes-letter.}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6877:   191	Two findings follow. First, the AUROC gain transfers: pooled AUROC moves from $0.781$ (Direct) to $0.811$ (agent$+$Direct ensemble), $+3.03$ pp with $95\%$ CI $[+1.48, +4.54]$ on a paired bootstrap---the same direction and (once labels are derived correctly) a somewhat larger magnitude than Table~\ref{tab:main_results}'s Qwen3.5 test gain. Second, MCQ accuracy does \emph{not} gain ($-0.8$ pp vs Direct). This is consistent with \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity}: averaging scores changes the \emph{rank order} between positive and negative items (helps AUROC) but rarely crosses the decision threshold at $0.5$ (does not help accuracy). A method that needs to improve threshold-based accuracy would need, in addition, a calibrated threshold or a decision rule that exploits the agent's disagreement with Direct near $0.5$; we leave that to future work. The important conclusion is that the ensemble mechanism is benchmark-agnostic at the \emph{ranking} level: on a completely independent benchmark, with different reference-template construction and class taxonomy, it delivers the same AUROC improvement at no inference-time re-tuning.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6879:   193	\subsection{Extending MMAD beyond AD: a unified task-aware agent (v9)}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6880:   194	\label{ssec:mmad_v9}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:6882:   196	The cross-benchmark experiment of \S\ref{ssec:mmad} only used the
