paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:8:We evaluate on \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11}, spanning twelve domain codes grouped into 11 reported families: D1 MVTec-AD industrial, D2 GoodsAD retail, D3 SDNET2018 concrete infrastructure (dev only), D4 DermaMNIST dermoscopy, D5 BraTS2021 brain MRI, D5b BMAD brain MRI, D5c BMAD liver CT, D5d HyperKvasir GI endoscopy variant, D6 LEVIR-CD+ remote-sensing change, D7 HyperKvasir GI endoscopy, D8 RA21+BDD road, D9 MVTec-LOCO logical, D10 VisA industrial. Each domain contributes 20 calibration / 40 dev / 120 test items (D8: 98 test). All items are reference-based, paired with 1--10 normal references. Total test items $n{=}1418$ (fusion strategies drop D8 items lacking SubspaceAD support to $n{=}1298$ and fall back to direct on the rest). The full per-domain specification is in Appendix~\ref{app:benchmark}.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:14:(i) \textbf{DINOv2-PatchNN}: patch-$k$-NN expert alone. (ii) \textbf{SubspaceAD}: PCA subspace expert alone~\citep{subspacead2026}. (iii) \textbf{Direct VLM} (v0): one VLM call with task-anchored descriptor. (iv) \textbf{Debate}: proposer--advocate, 2 VLM calls. (v) \textbf{Interpret}: AnomalyClaw v1 three-route router, $\sim 1.3$ VLM calls. (vi) \textbf{Fusion}: $s = 0.8\,s_0 + 0.2\,\sigma(s_\mathrm{SubspaceAD})$. (vii) \textbf{AnomalyClaw Descriptor Router}: per-domain rule $S(d)$ derived from the family taxonomy (\S\ref{ssec:router}; simulated by score look-up on the existing per-strategy outputs). (viii) \textbf{AnomalyClaw Calibration Router}: per-domain argmax strategy chosen from 20 calibration items per domain (simulated by score look-up). (ix) \textbf{AnomalyClaw v3 (real autonomous agent)}: end-to-end agent that runs the autonomous-planner VLM call per domain and executes the chosen tool/expert/strategy plan per item with the online expert override. (x) \textbf{Oracle}: per-domain argmax computed on test labels (upper bound; cheats).
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:17:Per-domain AUROC; macro-averaged across the 12 domain codes present in test. All thresholds and router assignments frozen from calibration. VLM anomaly scores extracted via structured JSON output (schema in Appendix~\ref{app:scoring}); $s = c$ if label is ``anomalous'' else $1 - c$. Bootstrap 95\% CIs via stratified paired bootstrap (per-domain stratification, $1{,}000$ resamples).
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:24:\caption{\textbf{Main results: per-domain AUROC across three VLM backbones} (test split, $n{=}1{,}418$). For each backbone we report the descriptor-only Direct VLM and our \emph{agent+Direct ensemble} ($s = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, weights frozen on dev). Agent choice per backbone: v6.5 on Qwen3.5/SeedVL, v6.6 self-ensemble on GPT-5.4 (selected on dev). \textbf{Macro} row at the bottom; $\Delta$ is ensemble macro minus Direct macro. 95\% paired bootstrap CIs on macro $\Delta$ are reported under the table; the gain is significant at $95\%$ on GPT-5.4 and Qwen3.5 ($P(\Delta\!>\!0)\!\ge\!1.000$), positive but not significant on SeedVL ($P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}0.927$).}
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:55:For reference, the descriptor-only Direct VLM and the SubspaceAD-fusion baseline (single call; $s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.2\,\sigma(s_\mathrm{SubspaceAD})$) have macro AUROC $0.855/0.808/0.814$ on GPT-5.4/SeedVL/Qwen3.5; the agent+Direct ensemble reaches $0.864/0.809/0.814$: $+0.87/+0.03/\pm 0$\,pp vs the expert-assisted fusion baseline, using \emph{no} external expert model. All backbones use the same \texttt{v6\_direct\_*} descriptor Direct (single canonical file per backbone; see Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}); an earlier SeedVL draft used a pre-v6 descriptor run (\texttt{seedvl\_v0\_direct\_test\_all\_v2.json}) which gave a systematically weaker Direct baseline ($0.7794$) and correspondingly a larger $\Delta$ ($+2.14$ pp); we rectify the provenance here. Per-backbone single-system agent results (v6.5 or v6.6 alone, no ensemble), v8 refutation ensemble, and the triple blend are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}.}
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:68:Replacing a generic domain descriptor with a task-anchored one significantly improves all three backbones at zero inference cost (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a): macro AUROC moves from 0.761 to 0.825 on GPT-5.4 ($+6.4$ pp, 95\% CI $[+4.4,+8.4]$), 0.748 to 0.789 on SeedVL ($+4.1$ pp, $[+1.6,+6.4]$), and 0.760 to 0.792 on Qwen3.5 ($+3.2$ pp, $[+1.0,+5.5]$). The gain concentrates in domains where VLM world-knowledge priors conflict with the task-specific anomaly definition (D08 LEVIR change, D06 BMAD liver CT); full per-backbone per-domain evidence is in Appendix~\ref{app:descriptor}. We recommend this as baseline practice for any training-free VAD system.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:71:Across 12 test domain codes, the argmax strategy differs by domain: fusion is preferred on 7--8 domains (industrial, retail, logical, liver CT, brain MRI), direct wins on 2--3 (brain MRI on SeedVL, LEVIR change, road), debate wins on 1--2 (road on GPT-5.4), and expert-only wins on 1 (dermoscopy via patch-$k$-NN). Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}(a) visualises this on SeedVL; the per-backbone tables are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. This strategy variance is the motivation for an agent rather than a single fixed recipe.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:73:\paragraph{Finding 3: A calibration-split router closes 40--60\% of the oracle gap.}
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:74:The calibration router (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}b) matches or beats every single-strategy baseline on every backbone. On SeedVL it beats fusion by \textbf{$+1.64$ pp} (95\% CI $[+0.02, +3.18]$, significant at $p{<}0.05$ by stratified paired bootstrap); it beats direct by $+3.27$ pp on SeedVL, $+0.97$ pp on GPT-5.4, and $+5.63$ pp on Qwen3.5 (all significant except GPT-5.4 $\to$ direct). The oracle upper bound leaves $+1.3$, $+1.3$, $+1.4$ pp of further headroom on the three backbones respectively. A zero-data \emph{descriptor} router (rules from the benchmark family taxonomy, no metrics consulted) is surprisingly competitive: $+1.7$ / $+2.2$ / $+4.9$ pp over direct on GPT-5.4 / SeedVL / Qwen3.5, all significant.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:84:Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$; one-sided bootstrap probability $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). Because the CI touches zero, we characterise this gain as positive-bootstrap rather than strictly significant. On GPT-5.4 dev (no test due to proprietary API rate-limit issues for concurrent v8 runs), the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). A minor schema fix was applied to the v8 runner after this test-set inference was completed; the reported v8 AUROC therefore reflects the pre-fix run. The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:89:We test candidate mechanisms for the ensemble gain through controlled post-hoc transformations of the v6.5 agent's scores on the existing Qwen3.5-VL-27B test set (no new inference). Each transformation is designed to change exactly one property and freeze the others, so a change in ensemble gain isolates that property's causal contribution.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:94:    \item \textbf{v6-AFFINE($a$)}: $s\mapsto\mathrm{clip}(0.5 + (s-0.5)\cdot a, 0, 1)$. Preserves rank for $a\!\le\!1$ (no clipping) and tests rank collapse near the boundaries for $a\!>\!1$.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:101:\caption{Controlled ablation on Qwen3.5-VL-27B test ($n{=}1418$, macro AUROC over 12 domains). Columns: the agent variant, its standalone AUROC, the $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}+0.5\!\cdot\!X$ ensemble AUROC, the \emph{number of unique score values}, the middle-zone mass, and the Spearman correlation with Direct. The key row is \textbf{v6-EXT-RANK}: middle-mass drops to $0\%$ but rank granularity is preserved, and the ensemble gain actually \emph{increases} to $+4.68$ pp. Conversely \textbf{v6-BIN} keeps sign-level direction but collapses ranks from $49$ to $2$, and the ensemble drops to $+2.31$ pp. Middle-mass is correlational with rank granularity on unprocessed outputs; rank granularity is what causes ensemble gain.}
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:127:The calibration router's choice coincides with the oracle on 9/12 domains for GPT-5.4 and 10/12 for both SeedVL and Qwen3.5. Mismatches concentrate where two strategies are within 1 pp on calibration (a bootstrap-level tie that the router cannot reliably resolve with 20 items), and the realised AUROC gap on test is small ($\le 0.06$). The per-backbone per-strategy per-domain table and the per-domain router-choice overlay are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain} (Figure~\ref{fig:per_domain_appendix}).
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:134:\caption{Stratified paired bootstrap summary (test split, $1{,}000$ resamples, per-domain stratification). $\Delta$ is the pairwise macro-AUROC gap; CI is the 95\% percentile interval; \checkmark\ means the CI excludes zero.}
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:144:GPT-5.4 & oracle vs fusion & $+0.013$ & $[+0.001, +0.026]$ & \checkmark \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:149:SeedVL & oracle vs calibration-router & $+0.013$ & $[+0.001, +0.025]$ & \checkmark \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:154:Qwen3.5 & oracle vs calibration-router & $+0.014$ & $[+0.006, +0.022]$ & \checkmark \\
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:159:Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap} summarises the stratified paired bootstrap for the headline comparisons. The calibration router strictly beats every single-strategy baseline on SeedVL (including fusion, significant); it ties fusion on GPT-5.4 and Qwen3.5 (fusion is the dominant single strategy on those backbones). Against the weaker \emph{direct} baseline that prior agentic-VAD work usually compares to, all three routers---descriptor, calibration, and oracle---show significant gains.
paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:191:Two findings follow. First, the AUROC gain transfers: pooled AUROC moves from $0.781$ (Direct) to $0.811$ (agent$+$Direct ensemble), $+3.03$ pp with $95\%$ CI $[+1.48, +4.54]$ on a paired bootstrap---the same direction and (once labels are derived correctly) a somewhat larger magnitude than Table~\ref{tab:main_results}'s Qwen3.5 test gain. Second, MCQ accuracy does \emph{not} gain ($-0.8$ pp vs Direct). This is consistent with \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity}: averaging scores changes the \emph{rank order} between positive and negative items (helps AUROC) but rarely crosses the decision threshold at $0.5$ (does not help accuracy). A method that needs to improve threshold-based accuracy would need, in addition, a calibrated threshold or a decision rule that exploits the agent's disagreement with Direct near $0.5$; we leave that to future work. The important conclusion is that the ensemble mechanism is benchmark-agnostic at the \emph{ranking} level: on a completely independent benchmark, with different reference-template construction and class taxonomy, it delivers the same AUROC improvement at no inference-time re-tuning.
