    68	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
    69	\STATE \textbf{Input:} query $x$, references $\mathcal{R}$, domain $d$, descriptor $\phi_d$, frozen plan $\pi(d) = (S, E)$.
    70	\STATE $s_E \leftarrow \mathrm{Expert}_E(x, \mathcal{R})$ \hfill\COMMENT{cached lookup; 0 VLM calls}
    71	\IF{$S = \text{expert\_only}$}
    72	    \STATE \textbf{return} $\sigma((s_E - m_E)/m_E)$ \hfill\COMMENT{0 VLM calls}
    73	\ENDIF
    74	\STATE $s_0 \leftarrow \mathrm{VLM}_{\mathrm{v0}}(x, \mathcal{R}, \phi_d)$ \hfill\COMMENT{1 VLM call}
    75	\IF{$S = \text{direct}$}
    76	    \STATE \textbf{return} $s_0$
    77	\ELSIF{$S = \text{fusion}(E)$}
    78	    \STATE \textbf{return} $(1 - w_d)\,s_0 + w_d\,\sigma((s_E - m_E)/m_E)$
    79	\ELSIF{$S = \text{zoom\_fusion}(E)$}
    80	    \STATE $c \leftarrow \mathrm{HotspotCropper}(x, \mathrm{Expert}_E.\text{patches})$
    81	    \STATE $s_0 \leftarrow \mathrm{VLM}_{\mathrm{v0}}(x, \mathcal{R}, \phi_d, c)$ \hfill\COMMENT{1 VLM call with crop}
    82	    \STATE \textbf{return} $(1 - w_d)\,s_0 + w_d\,\sigma((s_E - m_E)/m_E)$
    83	\ELSIF{$S = \text{debate}$}
    84	    \STATE \textbf{return} $\mathrm{Debate}(x, \mathcal{R}, \phi_d, s_0)$ \hfill\COMMENT{2 VLM calls}
    85	\ENDIF
    86	\end{algorithmic}
    87	\end{algorithm}
    88	
    89	\subsection{The v6 ReAct agent (headline system)}
    90	\label{ssec:v6}
    91	
    92	The v4 calibration router of \S\ref{ssec:router} is our framework; the
    93	paper's headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results} is produced by a
    94	different concrete instance, the \textbf{v6 ReAct agent} ensembled with
    95	Direct VLM. We describe v6 here to avoid confusion between the framework
    96	and the winning recipe.
    97	
    98	v6 is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop. On each image the VLM sees a
    99	one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a
   100	13-tool catalogue, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a
   101	JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly
   102	score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain: the
   103	router of \S\ref{ssec:router} is not used by the v6 agent at
   104	inference. Of the 13 tools, two invoke additional VLM or LLM calls
   105	inside the tool body (\texttt{tool\_reference\_profiler},
   106	\texttt{tool\_domain\_knowledge}); the other eleven are zero-VLM-call
   107	data transformations or cached expert lookups (see
   108	Appendix~\ref{app:cost} for the average measured call count per
   109	backbone).
   110	
   111	The v6 agent alone matches Direct on GPT-5.4 ($+1.1$ pp) and Qwen3.5
   112	($+0.3$ pp) but falls short on SeedVL ($-1.7$ pp). Our headline recipe
   113	is the simple arithmetic average with Direct (dev-frozen $\alpha=0.5$):
   114	$s_{\mathrm{final}} = 0.5\cdot s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\cdot s_{v6}$.
   115	The ensemble is what closes the remaining gap and produces the numbers
   116	in Table~\ref{tab:main_results} (\S\ref{ssec:score_diversity} explains
   117	\emph{why} ensembling works via score-rank granularity, not why v6 alone
   118	beats Direct---v6 alone does not always beat Direct).
   119	
   120	\subsection{Refutation agent (v8)}
   121	\label{ssec:refutation}
   122	
   123	Beyond the calibration-argmax planner, we introduce a deliberation protocol that directly targets two failure modes we observe in v6.5: (i) \emph{coarse rank grid}---the VLM emits scores drawn from a small set of unique values (11 distinct values on the Qwen3.5-VL test set), which limits ranking resolution and caps AUROC gains; (ii) \emph{confirmation bias}---tool calls tend to support the VLM's working hypothesis rather than test it.
   124	
   125	\paragraph{Protocol.}
   126	The refutation agent runs on top of the v6 ReAct loop but replaces the free-form ``call any tool, update score'' pattern with a structured three-phase schema:
   127	\begin{enumerate}
   128	    \item \textbf{Suspect list (turn 1, no tool).} The VLM outputs an \texttt{initial\_score}, a list of up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}~$=\{(\text{name}_i, \text{location\_hint}_i, \text{looks\_defect\_like}_i)\}$ and a \texttt{refutation\_target} index~$t^*$ into that list. The prompt fixes the default: if \texttt{candidate\_features} is empty and \texttt{initial\_score}~$<0.3$, the agent finalises at~$0.05$ without calling a tool.
   129	    \item \textbf{Targeted refutation (turn 2+).} The agent calls a refutation tool whose output is about \emph{whether the feature at $t^*$ appears in any reference}. \texttt{side\_by\_side} crops query and four refs at the same bbox; \texttt{reference\_profiler} returns a structured normal-baseline profile (object, colors, allowed variations); \texttt{reference\_retriever} fetches top-$k$ similar normals. Each output is paired with an explicit \texttt{interpretation} field containing a disconfirm clause, e.g.\ ``the bright region in the composite also appears in ref 2 at the same position.''
   130	    \item \textbf{Verdict and update.} The VLM emits \texttt{refutation\_verdict} $\in \{\text{found\_in\_ref}, \text{not\_found}, \text{inconclusive}\}$, removes refuted features from \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features}, and outputs \texttt{updated\_score}. If the remaining list is empty the score is forced into $[0.05, 0.20]$; if at least one feature survives the score is in $[0.40, 0.95]$ proportional to its defect-likeness. Remaining features are checked in subsequent turns until budget is exhausted (max 5 turns).
   131	\end{enumerate}
   132	
   133	\paragraph{Why this counters confirmation bias.}
   134	In v6.5, a tool was invoked to \emph{support} the VLM's working hypothesis and the system accumulated supporting observations. In v8, every tool output has exactly one legitimate use---retiring a candidate feature---so the protocol is naturally biased toward ruling anomalies \emph{out}. The structured output (\texttt{candidate\_features}, per-turn \texttt{refutation\_verdict}, \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features}) gives a reviewer an auditable trace.
   135	
   136	\paragraph{Ensemble with Direct.}
   137	Both v6.5 and v8 standalone sit below Direct on dev (dev macros $0.6942$ and $0.6865$ vs Direct $0.7599$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B). We therefore report the agents after averaging with the Direct VLM score at a single frozen weight selected on dev:
   138	\[
   139	  s_{\mathrm{final}} \;=\; w \cdot s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + (1-w) \cdot s_{\mathrm{agent}}
   140	\]
   141	with $w{=}0.5$ for the v6.5 ensemble and $w{=}0.6$ for the v8 ensemble. Section~\ref{sec:experiments} contains the ablation over $w$. A triple blend $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct} + 0.1\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.5} + 0.4\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ is also reported; all weights are frozen on dev before a single test query is seen.
   142	
   143	\paragraph{Rank-granularity mechanism.}
   144	Section~\ref{sec:experiments} isolates the causal driver of the ensemble gain via rank-preserving and rank-collapsing transformations. Direct emits only $11$ unique scores on our test set, v6.5's free-form JSON protocol emits $49$, and averaging the two fills in the rank ties inside Direct's coarse bimodal clusters with real signal. Transformations that preserve the agent's $49$ unique values---whether keeping the middle-zone mass at $24$\% (the original), inflating it to $100$\% (affine compression), or collapsing it to $0$\% (rank-preserving extreme remap)---all reach the same $+4.7$~pp ensemble gain. A median-split binarisation that collapses the $49$ values to $2$ cuts the gain in half. v8's explicit refutation schema, which assigns scores from a small vocabulary tied to the refutation-verdict decision, retains fewer unique values and therefore captures a smaller share of the ensemble gain. Designing an agent whose deliberation mechanism \emph{also} produces a fine rank grid is the main open direction left by this work.
   145	
   146	\subsection{Design principles}
   147	\label{ssec:principles}
   148	\begin{itemize}
   149	    \item \textbf{No training.} All VLMs and experts are frozen. SubspaceAD's PCA is a closed-form fit on reference features; patch-$k$-NN is a direct distance computation.
   150	    \item \textbf{No per-domain code.} The only per-domain artefact is the descriptor $\phi_d$ (one sentence) plus a family tag used by the descriptor router; both are written once.
   151	    \item \textbf{Bounded call budget.} At most 2 VLM calls per item; the average across CrossDomainVAD-11 is $\approx 1.3$.
   152	    \item \textbf{Auditable decisions.} Every output is accompanied by a trace listing the tools invoked, the expert selected, the chosen strategy, and (when applicable) the VLM's two-call evidence. Appendix~\ref{app:trace} shows a full trace example.
   153	\end{itemize}

