     1	\section{Benchmark Specification}
     2	\label{app:benchmark}
     3	
     4	Table~\ref{tab:benchmark_detail} provides per-domain details for CrossDomainVAD-11. All splits, item IDs, reference mappings, and domain descriptors will be released as a JSON specification file.
     5	
     6	\begin{table*}[t]
     7	\centering
     8	\caption{CrossDomainVAD-11 per-domain specification.}
     9	\label{tab:benchmark_detail}
    10	\footnotesize
    11	\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
    12	\begin{tabular}{cllp{0.40\linewidth}rl}
    13	\toprule
    14	ID & Domain & Source & Anomaly definition & Refs/item & License \\
    15	\midrule
    16	D01 & Industrial & MVTec-AD & Surface defect, damage, contamination & 10 & CC BY-NC-SA \\
    17	D02 & Retail & GoodsAD & Torn label, deformation, missing component & 10 & Research \\
    18	D03 & Infrastructure & SDNET2018 & Cracks, spalling, structural damage & 10 & Public \\
    19	D04 & Dermoscopy & DermaMNIST & Malignant melanoma & 10 & CC BY \\
    20	D05 & Brain MRI & BraTS2021 & Glioma, mass effect, oedema & 10 & CC BY \\
    21	D06 & Liver CT & BMAD-Liver & Focal lesion, tumour, cyst & 10 & Research \\
    22	D07 & Endoscopy & HyperKvasir & Polyp, ulcer, inflammation & 10 & Research \\
    23	D08 & Change Det. & LEVIR-CD+ & Building-level change (construction, demolition) & 1--2 & Research \\
    24	D09 & Road Obstacle & RA21+BDD & Unexpected object on roadway & 2 & Research \\
    25	D10 & Logical & MVTec-LOCO & Wrong count/arrangement of components & 10 & CC BY-NC-SA \\
    26	D11 & VisA & VisA & Surface defect, chips, scratches & 10 & CC BY-NC-SA \\
    27	\bottomrule
    28	\end{tabular}
    29	\end{table*}
    30	
    31	\paragraph{Reference selection.}
    32	References are normal images from the same category/scene, selected deterministically by metadata match then fixed random seed. No reference appears as a query. Patient-level (D04--D07) and event-level (D08) deduplication prevents leakage.
    33	
    34	\paragraph{Split protocol.}
    35	Per domain: 20 calibration, 40 dev, 120 test (balanced 50/50, except D08 LEVIR-CD+ with 98 test items). All splits frozen before experiments.
    36	
    37	\paragraph{Task-anchored domain descriptors.}
    38	The descriptor $d$ is a single sentence inserted into every VLM prompt. The eleven descriptors used in CrossDomainVAD-11 are:
    39	\begin{itemize}\small\setlength\itemsep{1pt}
    40	    \item \textbf{D01 Industrial (MVTec-AD):} Normal = a manufactured object matching the reference in geometry, texture, and colour. Anomaly = surface defect, damage, contamination, or missing/extra component.
    41	    \item \textbf{D02 Retail (GoodsAD):} Normal = a packaged supermarket product matching the reference in label, shape, and surface. Anomaly = torn label, deformation, missing component, or printing defect.
    42	    \item \textbf{D03 Infrastructure (SDNET2018):} Normal = intact concrete surface. Anomaly = crack, spalling, or structural damage.
    43	    \item \textbf{D04 Dermoscopy (DermaMNIST):} Normal = benign melanocytic skin lesion. Anomaly = malignant melanoma.
    44	    \item \textbf{D05 Brain MRI (BraTS2021):} Normal = healthy brain MRI slice. Anomaly = glioma, mass effect, or peritumoural oedema.
    45	    \item \textbf{D06 Liver CT (BMAD-Liver):} Normal = healthy liver CT slice. Anomaly = focal lesion, tumour, or cyst.
    46	    \item \textbf{D07 GI Endoscopy (HyperKvasir):} Normal = clean gastrointestinal mucosa. Anomaly = polyp, ulcer, or inflammation.
    47	    \item \textbf{D08 Change Detection (LEVIR-CD+):} Normal = no building change between reference and query. Anomaly = building-level change including new construction, demolition, or expansion. \emph{Planned urbanisation IS the anomaly}.
    48	    \item \textbf{D09 Road Obstacle (RA21+BDD):} Normal = empty driveable roadway (possibly with expected vehicles and markings). Anomaly = unexpected object on the roadway.
    49	    \item \textbf{D10 Logical (MVTec-LOCO):} Normal = correct count and spatial arrangement of components. Anomaly = wrong count, wrong position, or wrong component type.
    50	    \item \textbf{D11 VisA (VisA):} Normal = a manufactured object matching the reference. Anomaly = surface defect, chips, scratches, or missing component.
    51	\end{itemize}
    52	
    53	A corresponding generic baseline descriptor used in Finding~1 reads simply: ``\emph{Identify whether the query image is anomalous relative to the reference(s).}''
    54	
    55	\section{Score Extraction Rules}
    56	\label{app:scoring}
    57	
    58	All VLM variants output structured JSON. The anomaly score $s \in [0, 1]$:
    59	
    60	\paragraph{v0 Direct.}
    61	Output: \texttt{\{image\_label, confidence, anomaly\_type\}}.
    62	$s = c$ if label $=$ ``anomalous'', $s = 1{-}c$ if ``normal'', where $c$ is reported confidence. Parse failure rate: $<1\%$ across all backbones.
    63	
    64	\paragraph{AnomalyClaw interpret.}
    65	Same JSON schema and scoring. Final score: interpret output on escalated items, $s_0$ on committed items.
    66	
    67	\paragraph{Route B.}
    68	$s = 0.3 \cdot s_0 + 0.7 \cdot \sigma(\text{expert\_score})$, where $\sigma$ normalises relative to calibration median.
    69	
    70	\section{Failed agent variants}
    71	\label{app:ablation}
    72	
    73	Table~\ref{tab:ablation_appendix} reports the six alternative agent designs we tested on GPT-5.4 calibration. All were fixed and tuned on the same 220-item calibration split as AnomalyClaw. Failure analysis per variant:
    74	
    75	\begin{table}[h]
    76	\centering
    77	\caption{Ablation of agent variants (GPT-5.4, calibration macro AUROC). All use the same descriptor-enhanced v0 as the base.}
    78	\label{tab:ablation_appendix}
    79	\small
    80	\begin{tabular}{lcc}
    81	\toprule
    82	Variant & Macro & $\Delta$ vs v0 \\
    83	\midrule
    84	v0 Direct (baseline)     & 0.785 & --- \\
    85	v1 Normal-First          & 0.761 & $-$2.4 \\
    86	v2 Self-Refine           & 0.726 & $-$5.9 \\
    87	v3 Debate (naive score)  & 0.713 & $-$7.2 \\
    88	v3 Debate (gated score)  & 0.744 & $-$4.1 \\
    89	Evidence-grounded prop.  & 0.683 & $-$10.2 \\
    90	Third-call arbiter       & 0.694 & $-$9.1 \\
    91	\midrule
    92	\textbf{AnomalyClaw}     & \textbf{0.837} & \textbf{$+$5.2} \\
    93	\bottomrule
    94	\end{tabular}
    95	\end{table}
    96	
    97	\begin{itemize}
    98	    \item \textbf{Normal-First} (v1): structured prompting forces the VLM to enumerate ``normal'' claims, introducing false positives. On Qwen3.5, the reasoning chain consumes the token budget before emitting JSON.
    99	    \item \textbf{Self-Refine} (v2): a second non-adversarial pass adds no new information; it is the weakest variant, confirming that more calls $\neq$ better.
   100	    \item \textbf{Debate} (v3): a refuter rationalises confident correct predictions. Even with a confidence-gated aggregation rule, debate never beats v0.
   101	    \item \textbf{Evidence injection}: feeding DINOv2 patch distances into the proposer prompt distracts the VLM ($-10$ pp).
   102	    \item \textbf{Arbiter}: a third VLM call on contested items fires on 66\% of items (too aggressive) and is net negative.
   103	\end{itemize}
   104	
   105	These ablations were run on GPT-5.4 calibration because they represent disqualified designs rather than competitive baselines; we did not re-run them on SeedVL or Qwen3.5 once the failure pattern was clear.
   106	
   107	\section{Expert model comparison}
   108	\label{app:expert}
   109	
   110	We benchmarked three expert models on the calibration split (Table~\ref{tab:expert_appendix}). SubspaceAD dominates overall, reaching 1.000 AUROC on D11 VisA calibration where all VLMs score $\leq 0.85$. AnomalyVFM (zero-shot, DINOv2 + LoRA) is uniformly weaker (macro 0.599) and was excluded from AnomalyClaw. DINOv2-PatchNN complements SubspaceAD on D03 infrastructure (0.80 vs 0.50). The oracle that selects the best expert per domain reaches macro 0.898, indicating 7 pp headroom for multi-expert routing as future work.
