 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	Vision-language models (VLMs) have become the default tool for training-free visual anomaly detection (VAD), yet single-pass prompting fails silently on domains where the VLM's world-knowledge priors conflict with the task-specific anomaly definition. We study how to add structure on top of a VLM for \emph{cross-domain} VAD and present two complementary contributions.
     2	
     3	\textbf{Main finding.} On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items), a simple arithmetic ensemble of the VLM's Direct score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline $0.7684$ by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The ensemble recipe is $s_{\mathrm{final}} = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, \emph{frozen} on dev before any test query is seen. The recipe beats the stronger SubspaceAD \emph{fusion} baseline by $+0.9$ pp on GPT-5.4 and matches it on Qwen3.5, using only VLM outputs, no additional expert model.
     4	
     5	\textbf{Mechanism.} Controlled rank-preserving ablations localise the gain to \emph{score-rank granularity}, not to middle-zone mass. Direct emits only 11 unique score values on our test set whereas the v6 ReAct agent emits 49; the agent's finer rank grid breaks ties inside Direct's bimodal clusters when the two scores are averaged. A rank-preserving transformation that keeps the agent's 49 unique scores but pushes them all outside $[0.2, 0.8]$ preserves the ensemble gain ($+4.68$ pp) despite $0\%$ middle-zone mass; a rank-collapsing binarisation (two unique values) drops the gain to $+2.31$ pp; a random middle-mass noise partner (uniform in $[0.2, 0.8]$) gives $-3.4$ pp. Middle-zone mass is therefore correlated with ensemble gain only because it correlates with rank granularity; when we decouple them, granularity is what matters. The deeper ingredients are: (i) the agent's score has non-trivial standalone signal (random score with no signal hurts the ensemble); (ii) the agent has a distinct rank grid with many unique values; (iii) the agent's errors are complementary to the VLM's.
     6	
     7	\textbf{Secondary contribution.} We introduce a \emph{refutation agent} (v8) that implements an explicit ``normal until survived refutation'' protocol: the VLM lists candidate features, each is targeted by a refutation tool, surviving candidates yield anomaly evidence. v8 is interpretable---a reviewer can audit exactly which features survived and why---and gains $+1.28$ pp on Qwen3.5 test (CI $[-0.001,+0.027]$) when ensembled with Direct. Its smaller gain relative to v6 is explained by the same rank-granularity mechanism: v8's explicit refutation protocol collapses scores to a small number of unique values ($0.05, 0.85$) which preserves correct rank on refuted vs surviving features but destroys rank granularity within each group.
     8	
     9	\textbf{Takeaway.} Training-free VAD benefits from \emph{score-diverse} VLM-agent ensembling; the gain is explained by complementary rank structure, not by stronger standalone agents. We release the v6/v8 agent implementations, per-tool single-tool audit framework (13 tools, dev n=480), and full paired-bootstrap analysis artifacts.
    10	\section{Introduction}
    11	\label{sec:intro}
    12	
    13	Vision-language models (VLMs) have become the default tool for training-free visual anomaly detection (VAD): a practitioner uploads a query image, optionally attaches one or two normal reference images, and asks the model whether the query is abnormal. This recipe drives much of the recent published progress on industrial~\citep{gu2024anomalygpt,jeong2023winclip}, medical~\citep{bao2024bmad}, and cross-domain~\citep{jiang2024mmad} benchmarks. The appeal is obvious: no training, no architecture changes, and a single VLM call per image.
    14	
    15	\begin{figure}[t]
    16	    \centering
    17	    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_intuition.pdf}
    18	    \caption{\textbf{AnomalyClaw on CrossDomainVAD-11.}
    19	    \textbf{(a)} No single strategy wins uniformly across domains: the best direct/fusion/debate/interpret cell differs by family (industrial, medical, logical, GI, change, road). \textbf{(b)} A calibration-driven router matches or beats the strongest single strategy on all three backbones and captures 40--60\% of the oracle gap; a zero-data descriptor router recovers much of the gain for free. \textbf{(c)} Per-domain strategy assignment is well-aligned with the oracle (9--10/12 agreement across backbones).}
    20	    \label{fig:intuition}
    21	\end{figure}
    22	
    23	Two observations drive this work. First, a cross-domain VAD deployment must handle textural industrial defects, semantic road obstacles, brain-MRI lesions, and infrastructure cracks with the same pipeline. The same fixed recipe---no matter how clever---cannot be optimal on all of them simultaneously: we find that on our 12-domain test split, the argmax strategy varies by domain family and gains of up to $+3$ pp macro AUROC are available simply by switching strategy per domain (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a). Second, recent ``agentic VAD'' systems~\citep{zhang2025agentiad,chen2026eagle,wei2025autoiad} bundle three decisions---what the VLM attends to, which expert provides evidence, and how to aggregate---into a single monolithic pipeline that is hard to ablate and hard to compare against simpler baselines.
    24	
    25	We argue that these three decisions should be explicit and decoupled. We introduce \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free VAD agent organised around three orthogonal axes:
    26	
    27	\begin{itemize}
    28	    \item \textbf{Tools} --- zero-VLM-call primitives (domain descriptor, reference retriever, hotspot cropper, component counter, knowledge lookup).
    29	    \item \textbf{Experts} --- frozen, pre-computed, non-parametric detectors (SubspaceAD~\citep{subspacead2026}, DINOv2 patch-$k$-NN, DINOv2 global).
    30	    \item \textbf{Strategies} --- inference schemes that combine tools, experts, and VLM calls (direct, fusion, debate, interpret).
    31	\end{itemize}
    32	
    33	A lightweight \emph{router} selects a (tools, expert, strategy) triple per image. The router comes in two flavours: \emph{descriptor rules} (one rule per domain family, frozen once from the benchmark descriptor, no labelled data) and \emph{calibration argmax} (per-domain argmax over 20 labelled calibration items). An online expert-signal override can promote any domain to the \emph{interpret} strategy when patch-$k$-NN detects a spatially concentrated anomaly that the VLM missed.
    34	
    35	\paragraph{Contributions.}
    36	\begin{enumerate}
    37	    \item \textbf{Score-diverse VLM-agent ensembling is the headline method.} A simple arithmetic average $s_{\mathrm{final}}{=}0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}}+0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$ reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B test ($n{=}1{,}418$, 12 domains), beating descriptor-only Direct by $+4.53$~pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The recipe matches SubspaceAD fusion on Qwen3.5 and edges it by $+0.9$~pp on GPT-5.4, using only VLM outputs.
    38	    \item \textbf{Rank-granularity mechanism.} Controlled rank-preserving ablations localise the gain to the number of unique values the agent emits, not to score middle-zone mass. Preserving $49$ unique values while removing all middle-zone mass keeps the full gain ($+4.68$~pp); collapsing to two unique values halves it ($+2.31$~pp); random middle-mass noise without signal hurts ($-3.39$~pp).
    39	    \item \textbf{Refutation agent (v8) as interpretable variant.} A ``normal until survived refutation'' protocol produces auditable per-item traces and gains $+1.28$~pp on Qwen3.5 test when ensembled with Direct. Its smaller gain is consistent with its coarser rank grid, connecting deliberation-protocol design to the rank-granularity mechanism.
    40	    \item \textbf{Task-anchored domain descriptors.} Descriptors significantly improve all three backbones at zero inference cost ($+6.4/+4.1/+3.2$ pp on GPT-5.4, SeedVL, Qwen3.5-VL-27B; paired bootstrap, $n{=}1{,}418$, all 95\% CIs exclude zero).
    41	    \item \textbf{Benchmark and framework release.} We release \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domain codes, $1{,}418$ test items, calibration/dev/test splits), the Tool/Expert/Strategy registry, the calibration-argmax router, the v8 refutation implementation with full trace logging, a 13-tool single-tool audit on Qwen3.5 dev, six failed-variant ablations, and stratified paired bootstrap confidence intervals for every headline comparison.
    42	\end{enumerate}
    43	\section{Conclusion}
    44	\label{sec:conclusion}
    45	
    46	We introduced \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free visual anomaly detection framework whose headline method is score-diverse VLM-agent ensembling: a simple arithmetic average of a Direct VLM score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score. On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} ($1{,}418$ test items, 12 domains) the ensemble reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating descriptor-only Direct by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). On GPT-5.4 it reaches $0.864$, edging the SubspaceAD-fusion baseline by $+0.9$ pp while using no external expert model. We localise the mechanism to \emph{score-rank granularity}: Direct emits $11$ unique scores on the test set, the agent emits $49$; averaging breaks ties inside Direct's coarse bimodal clusters with the agent's finer rank information. Controlled rank-preserving transformations confirm this: $0\%$ middle-mass with $49$ unique values still delivers $+4.68$ pp, whereas $0\%$ middle-mass with only $2$ unique values delivers $+2.31$ pp.
    47	
    48	We also introduced a \emph{refutation agent} (v8) that instantiates a ``normal until survived refutation'' protocol: the VLM lists candidate features, a refutation tool checks each against the reference pool, surviving features count as anomaly evidence. v8 ensembled with Direct gains $+1.28$ pp on Qwen3.5 test (positive one-sided bootstrap probability $0.962$; 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$ touches zero). v8 is positioned as an interpretable variant, not the main method: it produces per-item traces (candidate list, refutation verdict per tool call, remaining candidates) that a reviewer can audit, at the cost of $3.3$ pp relative to the plain $0.5$-average v6 ensemble. The smaller v8 gain is consistent with its coarser rank grid, closing the loop between deliberation-protocol design and the rank-granularity mechanism.
    49	
    50	Supporting findings include: task-anchored domain descriptors dominate any agentic intervention at zero inference cost ($+6.4/+4.1/+3.2$ pp on the three backbones, all significant); a calibration-argmax router beats every single-strategy baseline on every backbone; and six failed-variant ablations show that \emph{unconditional} second-call escalation hurts when the baseline is already strong.
    51	
    52	\paragraph{Limitations.}
    53	The v6 ensemble's macro-AUROC gain over SubspaceAD fusion is positive only on GPT-5.4 ($+0.9$ pp) and is tied on Qwen3.5 ($\pm 0$ pp). The refutation agent's Qwen3.5 test gain is positive-bootstrap but its CI touches zero; its stored test JSON was produced before a minor schema fix (corrected code is released; re-run not executed). GPT-5.4 v8 test at scale repeatedly rate-limits our proprietary API gateway, so GPT-5.4 numbers for v8 are dev-only ($n{=}480$). The v8 ensemble claim therefore generalises across backbones only at dev-split resolution, not test. The agent+Direct recipe matches the SubspaceAD-fusion baseline on Qwen3.5 rather than strictly outperforming it; we report it as a VLM-only alternative that avoids a domain-specialised expert.
    54	
    55	\paragraph{Future work.}
    56	Three directions follow from the rank-granularity mechanism: (i) deliberation protocols designed for a fine rank grid (e.g.\ requiring the VLM to output continuous refutation-strength scores rather than verdict categories) should improve v8's ensemble gain; (ii) a principled calibration-split ensemble weight selector per backbone, rather than our dev-frozen $w$; (iii) extension of the refutation protocol to operate on multiple candidate-feature lists produced from different prompt seeds, combining internal hypothesis diversity with our cross-system ensemble.

